"There is a difference of opinion among scholars as to whether the disjointed letters (huruf al-muqatta'at) found at the beginning of surahs are considered verses (ayahs) or not. While the Kufan scholars count these letters as independent verses, the Basran scholars do not accept any of them as a verse. This disagreement stems from the fact that verses are determined by divine decree (tawqifi) rather than analogy (qiyasi), and it is similar to variations in recitation (qira'ah). The first of the disputed verses of Surah al-Baqarah is \"Alif Lam Mim\".\n\nAlthough \"Alif Lam Mim\" appears as a single word in writing, it is pronounced as three distinct letters when recited. These letters are the names of the alphabetical letters that form the fundamental building blocks of language. Each letter signifies a sound and the written form of that sound. On their own, these letters are meaningless sounds, but by coming together, they form words and meanings. This situation brings to mind two different modes of reading and interpreting: the standard alphabet (alifba) or the numerical alphabet (abjad).\n\nExegetes (mufassirun) are divided into two main views regarding the meaning of these letters: According to the first view, Allah challenges the Arabs with these letters, emphasizing that they cannot produce the like of the Quran, which is composed of the very basic letters of the language they speak. Proponents of this view have also stated that \"Alif Lam Mim\" is a name of the surah or of the Quran. According to the second view, these letters serve as abbreviations or ciphers, pointing to specific meanings. For example, each letter could be the initial letter of a name or carry a numerical value according to abjad calculation. A third view combines both approaches, arguing that the letters possess both exoteric (zahir) and esoteric (batin) meanings.\n\nThe profound meaning of these letters takes a person on an intellectual journey leading from sounds to words, from words to meanings, from meanings to existence, and ultimately to Allah's knowledge. At the end of this journey, one must admit their helplessness and say, \"Allahu a'lam\" (Allah knows best). It is in this respect that the disjointed letters are among the allegorical verses (mutashabihat); that is, they are not devoid of meaning, but rather an expression whose full depth human intellect cannot completely comprehend because they are so rich in meaning.\n\nOne of the wisdoms behind the use of these letters at the beginning of surahs is to draw attention and evoke astonishment. According to narration, while the polytheists tried to prevent the recitation of the Quran by making noise, they began to listen in astonishment upon hearing these letters and thus benefited from the Quran. For this reason, these letters should especially be recorded as a reason for revelation (sabab al-nuzul). As for the absence of these letters at the beginning of al-Fatihah, it is because the primary aim of al-Fatihah is clear guidance and instruction.\n\nIn terms of grammatical inflection (i'rab), the names \"Alif Lam Mim\" have no grammatical inflection because they are words enumerated one by one. Nevertheless, they can be regarded as a subject-predicate (mubtada'-khabar) construction or as the direct object (maf'ul) of a verb. The sounds of these letters are divine sounds possessing distinctive qualities known only to the Prophet (peace be upon him), having resonated in his ear in a special way during revelation. These sounds have also been regarded as a name for the Quran or the surah.\n\nThe expression \"That is the Book,\" which follows these letters, introduces the Quran revealed with these extraordinary sounds. Here, the word \"book\" denotes both the wording (composition/nazm) and the meaning together. The Quran does not consist merely of meaning; rather, this unique composition that Allah revealed to our Prophet (peace be upon him) is a book that is both recited and written. Among Muslims, when \"the book\" is mentioned, the Noble Quran is the first thing that comes to mind.\n\nAt the beginning of Surah al-Baqarah, Elmalılı emphasizes that the Quran is a book in which there is no doubt (rayb). Rayb encompasses not only doubt, but also ill suspicion and accusation. With this verse, Allah has removed both intellectual and moral doubt, demonstrating the perfection of the book. Addressing the Prophet (peace be upon him), it was communicated that he should have no anxiety in this regard, and his trustworthiness in both receiving and conveying the revelation was confirmed.\n\nThose who doubt the truth of the Quran fall into two categories: either stubborn disbelievers combined with ignorance, or hypocrites who have made doubt a habitual disposition in everything. Their doubts are entirely baseless and unjustified. Subsequent verses (such as al-Baqarah 2:23 and al-An'am 6:93) will defend and substantiate this matter in greater detail.\n\nIn terms of theology (kalam) and philosophy, this beginning points to epistemology (theory of knowledge). In Islamic creed (aqa'id), the principle 'the realities of things are firmly established' refutes the Sophists (skeptics). The Quran is the greatest remedy against both theoretical and moral skepticism. The greatest ailment of humanity is doubt, and by eliminating it, the Quran demonstrates the true path.\n\nThe Book is guidance for the God-fearing (muttaqun). Guidance (hidayah) is used in two senses: showing the way and conveying one to the destination. The Quran's guidance is related to direction and counsel (irshad); whereas granting success (tawfiq) is an act of Allah. The Quran was sent down as a general guidance for everyone, yet benefiting from it depends on human will and the capacity for piety (taqwa). The God-fearing are those who possess the capacity for self-restraint and caution, and they are the ones who will receive the true share of guidance.\n\nPiety (taqwa/ittiqa) has three degrees: the first is to believe by shunning polytheism (shirk); the second is to avoid major sins and fulfill obligatory duties (fara'id); the third is to attain complete devotion by detaching the heart from everything that distracts it from Allah. This final degree is the lofty rank attained also by the prophets. Taqwa is not merely a negative abstinence, but also an active embrace of righteous deeds. The guidance of the Quran encompasses all of these degrees.\n\nThe first four verses of Surah al-Baqarah are like the answer to the prayer 'Guide us' in al-Fatihah. Through four harmonious sentences, these verses articulate the Quran's eternality, its truths, its epistemic character, and its objective of guidance. Ascending from three letters to three names, and from there to three concise sentences, this composition is a beautiful example of the harmony within the Quran.\n\nOne of the attributes of the God-fearing is belief in the unseen (ghayb). Ghayb is the realm that cannot be seen yet is real. Everything we perceive with our senses is, in fact, a manifestation of unseen movements. As Imam al-Ghazali also stated, things like light and sound are not separate external entities, but occurrences created at the moment of contact with our senses. Reality is grasped through intellect and inner insight (basirah).\n\nAllah Almighty is not the absolute unseen; He is the source of all things seen and unseen. He will be seen with the eyes in the Hereafter, yet He cannot be fully comprehended. This is the creed of the Ahl al-Sunnah. The vision of Allah will occur transcendent of direction and spatial location, nor will seeing Him constitute total comprehension.\n\nThe word 'ghayb' mentioned in the Quran means not only that which is unseen, but also that which has not yet been seen. We Muslims believe in the unseen that is supported by evidence and aligned with reason. For example, the past and the future are invisible today, but the future may be seen tomorrow. Belief in Allah, the angels, and the Hereafter is likewise; there are rational and traditional proofs for their existence.\n\nThe world is like a narrow isthmus between the past and the future. At present, man perceives only the events passing through this isthmus. Yet there are also realms of the unseen in the past and the future. Beyond these lies a supreme power that encompasses all things. Thus, the God-fearing (those mindful of Allah) believe in the Lord of these seen and unseen realms.\n\nThere are two types of the unseen: The first consists of unseen realities for which there is no evidence and which are known only to Allah. The second consists of unseen realities for which evidence exists, which are intended by the verse's statement, 'they believe in the unseen.' These are the articles of faith, such as Allah, the Hereafter, the angels, the prophets, and the scriptures. This faith is attained sometimes through intuitive discernment and experiential unveiling (kashf), and sometimes through rational proofs.\n\nThe word ghayb (the unseen) derives from the root ghaybah (absence from sight). Gha'ib (absent), on the other hand, refers to that which is not present, neither appearing to you nor seeing you. Ghayb, however, cannot be seen, yet it sees. When ghayb is applied to Allah, it is because He cannot be seen, yet He sees all things.\n\nIn lexical terms, faith (iman) means 'to feel secure' and 'to affirm'. As a religious term, it is to affirm with the heart and declare with the tongue the oneness of Allah, the prophethood of the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him), and the definitive knowledge he brought. The most concise expression of this faith is the declaration of faith (kalimah al-tawhid), while its broader expression is to believe in Allah, His angels, His books, His messengers, the Last Day, divine decree (qadar), and resurrection after death.\n\nIslamic scholars have advanced differing views regarding the nature of faith. According to the Kharijites and the Mu'tazilites, faith consists of heart, tongue, and deed. According to the Karramiyyah, it is solely declaration with the tongue. The Ash'arites take affirmation of the heart as the essential basis and make declaration with the tongue a requisite condition. The Hanafis, however, define faith as affirmation of the heart and declaration with the tongue; they regard deeds not as an integral part of faith, but as its fruit.\n\nAccording to Imam al-A'zam Abu Hanifa, faith is declaration with the tongue and affirmation of the heart. Mere verbal declaration would have necessitated accepting the faith of the hypocrites; mere knowledge, on the other hand, would have necessitated accepting the faith of the People of the Book. Yet Allah has revealed that the hypocrites are liars and that the People of the Book did not believe despite knowing the truth. As for deeds, they are separate from faith; they serve the perfection of faith. A Muslim cannot conceal his faith unless under compulsion; however, in situations of mortal danger, he may practice precautionary dissimulation (taqiyyah).\n\nThe foremost attribute of the god-fearing (muttaqin), who guard against disobeying Allah, is that they establish prayer. That is, not merely performing the prayer, but establishing it uprightly, fulfilling its conditions, observing it with reverence (khushu'), and maintaining it steadfastly. This serves as a reminder that prayer is the pillar of religion. When performed in congregation, prayer is like a column that sustains society.\n\nThe benefits of prayer are innumerable: purity, strength of heart, the ordering of time, and social rectification, among others. In particular, prostration shatters arrogance and instills humility in a person. Prayer is an ascension (mi'raj) that lifts the believer from human frailties and elevates him toward Allah.\n\nThe word salah signifies supplication and physical movement (moving the haunches). In Islam, prayer is a comprehensive act of worship performed with the heart, tongue, and body, possessing specific conditions and essential pillars. It has been detailed in the books of jurisprudence (fiqh) along with its obligations (fard), necessary duties (wajib), sunnah practices, and other rulings.\n\nThe god-fearing do not confine themselves to bodily acts of worship alone; they also spend from their wealth. Provision (rizq) encompasses everything from which benefit is derived, while spending (infaq) means expending in the path of Allah. At the forefront of this stands zakah. Zakah is the bridge of Islam and the foremost representative of financial acts of worship.\n\nZakah bridges the chasm between the rich and the poor, establishing bonds of love and brotherhood in society. When giving zakah, a Muslim regards himself as a distributor appointed by Allah. In this way, prayer and zakah, as the primary practices of faith, form the essence of all other acts of worship.\n\nThe god-fearing believe both in what has been revealed to you (the Quran) and in the scriptures revealed before you. This necessitates faith in all of Allah's prophets. Islam stands as a witness over all heavenly religions and confirms previous scriptures. In this respect, Islam is a universal and comprehensive religion.\n\nWhile most past religions granted no right of existence to anyone outside themselves, Islam approaches everyone with tolerance. In our times, humanity suffers under the claim of asserting dominance over truth. Salvation, however, is possible only by submitting to the absolute sovereignty of the True Lord (al-Haqq). Indeed, the heart of Islam moves with this broad conscience and faith.\n\nThose who will possess certain knowledge regarding the Hereafter are exclusively the god-fearing who possess this expansive faith. The conjectures about the Hereafter held by those who believe in some prophets and reject others, such as the Jews and Christians, can never attain the degree of certainty (yaqin). For the Hereafter is among the matters of the unseen (ghayb) and can only be known through the truthful reports of the prophets sent by Allah. The one who completed this report is the final Prophet, Muhammad (peace be upon him). The belief in the Hereafter of those who believe in past prophets but do not believe in the final Prophet and the scripture and Sacred Law revealed to him likewise does not conform to truth. Such corrupted beliefs can only be rectified through faith in the proclamations of Muhammad (peace be upon him), whereby true and certain knowledge of the Hereafter is attained.\n\nThe life of the Hereafter is entirely distinct from the life of this world, and the scientific laws of this world are not suitable for grasping all of its subtleties. The blessings of the Hereafter are eternal and devoid of sorrow; while some resemble those of this world, there are also things that no eye has seen, no ear has heard, and no human heart has conceived. Knowing the reality of the Hereafter depends upon knowing the foundation of truth. Science and knowledge demonstrate that the Hereafter is not a rationally absurd impossibility, that today has a tomorrow, and that we must prepare for it. Yet what that tomorrow will be like is known only to Allah, and can only be reported by the prophets who are granted knowledge of the unseen.\n\n'Iqan (certainty / yaqin) means definitive knowledge that conforms to reality and is unadulterated by doubt. Certainty has degrees: mathematical and logical knowledge is certain and necessary; empirical sciences (such as chemistry and physics), though not necessary, are certain. However, particular opinions derived through erroneous deductions from these sciences are not certain. Certainty is not confined merely to necessary and intuitive knowledge; what is witnessed, experienced, transmitted via mass corroboration (tawatur), and arrived at through sound deductive reasoning also yields certainty. Our firm conviction regarding Allah's power must never be abandoned while pursuing the sciences, nor should necessary certainties be sacrificed for the sake of ordinary certainties. Everything is possible by Allah's leave; in order not to be bewildered when confronted with the emergence of things previously deemed impossible, we must hold firmly to the principles of definitive faith.\n\n'Muflihun' derives from the root falah; it means cleaving through obstacles to attain deliverance and achieve what is desired—that is, those who attain salvation. Here, through the separating pronoun (damir al-fasl) 'hum', it is understood that muflihun is designated exclusively for the god-fearing who abide upon guidance. That is to say, salvation belongs solely to those who fear Allah, believe, and perform righteous deeds. For those who believe yet abandon acts of worship, their salvation is neither complete nor perfect; nevertheless, on account of their faith, one does not despair of Allah's mercy. This verse shows that salvation (falah) depends on guidance (hidayah), and guidance depends on mindfulness of Allah (taqwa); this dependency is not an inevitable causal relationship of necessity, but a manifestation of Allah's grace and mercy.\n\nLexically, kufr (disbelief) means to cover a blessing—that is, ingratitude. In religion, it is the antithesis of faith (iman) and entails denying, rejecting, or failing to affirm that which must be believed. Disbelief may be of the heart, verbal, or practical. Practical disbelief consists of committing acts incompatible with faith, such as worshipping idols or insulting the Quran. The abandonment of deeds, on the other hand, is of two types: partial abandonment (occasional omission) may not constitute disbelief; however, completely abandoning deeds or making the neglect of prayer a habit can constitute disbelief. Faith requires indivisible wholeness; when a part of it is rejected, disbelief occurs. There is no middle ground between faith and disbelief; a person is either a believer or a disbeliever.\n\nAllah has sealed the hearts and hearing of those who disbelieve, and over their eyes is a veil. This sealing (khatm) signifies that they will not accept guidance. 'Khatm' means to seal and solidify something or to bring it to an end. This verse announces that those who persist in disbelief will no longer find the right path and will suffer a tremendous punishment. While the preceding verses describe guidance and salvation for the God-fearing (muttaqin), here the condition of the disbelievers is explained by way of contrast.\n\nThe concept of the heart carries both physical and spiritual meanings. The physical heart is the pinecone-shaped piece of flesh located in the chest that enables blood circulation. The spiritual heart, on the other hand, is the center of consciousness, conscience, feeling, and thought. In the Quran and Islamic sciences, when the heart is mentioned, it is generally this second meaning that is intended. Faith, disbelief, and knowing Allah are related to this heart. Sealed hearts are the hearts of those who cannot perceive the truth and are trapped in their own desires.\n\nAllah has sealed the hearts and hearing of those whose disbelief has become manifest, and placed a veil over their eyes. Therefore, whether you warn them or do not warn them, they will not believe. In the verse, hearts and eyes are mentioned in the plural, whereas hearing is brought in the singular. The reason for this is an indication that in faith, the paths of reflection and sight are many, while the path of hearing is singular (the center of prophethood). These people are now deprived of all three means of knowledge required to comprehend the truth: heart and intellect, sound senses, and hearing the report.\n\nThe sealing of the heart occurs when sins become a habit. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: 'When a sin is committed for the first time, a black spot forms on the heart. If the person repents and turns to Allah, the heart becomes polished. When the sin is repeated, the stain increases and eventually covers the entire heart.' This is indeed the seal mentioned in the verse. This seal is a second nature acquired through the servant's own earning. Its creation belongs to Allah, yet it does not come to pass without the servant's earning. This point explains the secret of the matter of morality and upbringing.\n\nThese verses also shed light on the subject of predestination and compulsion. Allah's eternal knowledge does not compel a person. Although Allah knew that the disbelievers would not believe, He held them responsible for having faith. For, by virtue of their initial creation, they had the capacity to believe. However, after acquiring the habit of disbelief, they become incapable of doing so. This responsibility is according to the initial creation. There is no compulsion here; according to the Ahl al-Sunnah, the creation of actions belongs to Allah, while their acquisition belongs to the servant.\n\nThe occasion of revelation for these verses is the Jews and hypocrites in Medina. Among the Jews, just as there were those who believed, such as Abdullah ibn Salam, there were also those who persisted in obstinacy. The hypocrites, on the other hand, were certain individuals from the Aws and Khazraj tribes, whose leader was Abdullah ibn Ubayy. They were outwardly Muslim while harboring hostility within. For this reason, the verses at the beginning of Surah al-Baqarah introduce three groups of people: believers, disbelievers, and hypocrites.\n\nThe hypocrites say, 'We have believed in Allah and the Last Day,' though they do not believe. They seek to deceive Allah and the believers, yet they deceive none but themselves. In their hearts is a disease, so Allah has increased their disease. When it is said to them, 'Do not cause corruption on the earth,' they say, 'We are only peacemakers.' In truth, they are the very corruptors themselves. When they are asked to believe as the believers have believed, they say, 'Shall we believe as those fools have believed?' In truth, they themselves are the fools, but they do not know.\n\nWhen the hypocrites meet the believers, they say, 'We believe,' but when they are alone with their devils (their disbelieving friends), they say, 'We are with you; we were only mocking them.' Allah mocks them and gives them respite to wander blindly in their transgression. They have purchased error in exchange for guidance; their transaction has brought no profit, nor have they been guided. Their situation is like that of someone who kindles a fire: just as it illuminates his surroundings, Allah takes away their light and leaves them in darkness. Or they are like those caught in a rainstorm pouring from the sky, accompanied by darkness, thunder, and lightning; they thrust their fingers into their ears in terror of the thunderbolts. Allah encompasses them at every moment. Had He willed, He could have completely taken away their hearing and sight.\n\nConsciousness (shu'ur) in human beings is of two kinds: overt feeling and covert feeling. Overt feeling relates to the external senses, while covert feeling relates to conscience. Conscience is a kind of 'consciousness of consciousness.' Intellect, however, begins behind consciousness and deals with its prior state, its posterior state, and its inner reality. Consciousness is an instantaneous and simple state of unity, whereas the intellect engages in the synthesis and analysis of this consciousness. In the hearts of the hypocrites, there is an unprecedented spiritual disease.\n\nThis disease is that of disbelief, doubt, and hypocrisy. The hypocrites doubt everything, even themselves. They imagine truth and reality to consist solely of themselves. For this reason, they regard deceit and trickery as a mark of superiority. Yet this disease is curable; however, they do not hold fast to the true religion and doubt it as well.\n\nAllah Almighty has increased this disease. For they rejected the truth brought by the prophet and became obstinate. Because of this, Allah firmly entrenched the disease in their hearts and increased it more and more. For that reason, they began resorting to trickery against Allah and the believers. In the Hereafter, this disease will cause them a most painful punishment.\n\nWhen it is said to the hypocrites, 'Do not cause corruption on the earth,' they say, 'We are only reformers.' In truth, they are the very corruptors themselves, but they are not aware of it. They mistake the corruption they commit for reform. Furthermore, when it is said, 'Believe as these people have believed,' they refuse to accept equality, saying, 'Shall we believe as those fools have believed?'\n\nWhereas in reality, they themselves are the fools (sufaha'), yet they do not know it. The hypocrites consider faith foolishness and look down upon the believers. Yet knowledge is a matter of certainty (yaqin); it is attained not through doubt, but through true faith. They, however, are filled with doubt from beginning to end, and for this reason possess neither consciousness nor knowledge.\n\nWhen the hypocrites meet the believers, they flatter them saying, 'We believe,' but when they meet secretly with their devils (devils among humans and jinn), they say, 'We are with you; we are only mocking them.' According to a narration, when Abdullah ibn Ubayy saw Abu Bakr, Umar, and Ali, he praised them and then told his companions, 'This is how we ward off the fools'; whereupon this verse was revealed. The verse exposes the inner reality of the hypocrites and the covert forces behind them (the polytheist leaders and Jewish rabbis).\n\nThe word shaytan (devil) is a generic noun used for evil, obstinate beings that exceed all bounds in transgression. It has categories such as human devils, animal devils, and jinn devils. According to Sibawayh, it derives from the meaning of 'remoteness' (remote from truth), while according to another view, it means 'burned' or 'futile.' The human devil is visible, but his deviltry is hidden; the jinn devil, however, is a completely concealed evil force. The devils mentioned in the verse, in addition to the leaders of the hypocrites, are the polytheist leaders and Jewish scholars.\n\nAllah mocks the hypocrites; this mockery takes the form of sentencing them in this world to suffer inner torment while being treated as Muslims, and in the Hereafter to be disgraced in the lowest depths of the Fire. Divine mockery is a humiliation, yet it embodies justice; Allah turns them into a laughingstock by leaving them unchecked in their transgression. This verse was revealed to calm the agitation of the believers and to teach patience.\n\nThe hypocrites have purchased misguidance in exchange for guidance (the right path); this trade of theirs has brought no profit. For guidance is the capital of all capitals; to lose it and acquire error is to destroy both capital and profit. In contrast to the righteous, this verse shows the end of the hypocrites and emphasizes the importance of adhering to the law of honesty in trade. The two parables that follow were revealed to explain this state better.\n\nThe first parable: The state of the hypocrites resembles a man who kindles a fire; when the fire illuminates his surroundings, Allah takes away their light of sight and leaves them in darkness. They become deaf, dumb, and blind; they can never find the right path again. This symbolizes the blindness of the hypocrites' inner sight in the face of the illumination of the Prophet's call.\n\nThe second parable: The hypocrites are like people caught in a downpour falling from the sky. Within the rain are darkness, thunder, and lightning. Out of fear of death, they thrust their fingers into their ears. Allah encompasses the disbelievers from every side. This parable describes the dread and flight that the warnings and glad tidings of the Quran evoke in the hypocrites.\n\nThe state of the hypocrites is likened to a rainstorm laden with thunder and lightning. They thrust their fingers into their ears for fear of death, fleeing from the thunderclaps. Yet Allah encompasses the disbelievers on all sides; there is no escaping His punishment. The One Who must truly be feared is Allah, and true duty lies in obeying His commands.\n\nThunder, lightning, and thunderbolts are actually three distinct manifestations of a single phenomenon. According to the religious explanation, there is an angel who drives the clouds, called 'ra'd' (thunder). This angel glorifies Allah, and as he shouts, he casts fire from his mouth; lightning is thus these whips of fire, and the thunderbolt is his strike. All of these occurrences simply consist of executing Allah's command.\n\nNatural sciences also explain these phenomena through electrical discharge. When positively and negatively charged clouds come together, lightning and thunder are produced. These explanations do not contradict the angelic force described by religion; on the contrary, they demonstrate the existence of a motive force. Both explanations essentially point to the same reality: Allah's power.\n\nWhenever lightning flashes, the hypocrites walk in its light, but when darkness descends upon them, they stand completely still. Had Allah willed, He would have taken away their hearing and their sight as well. For Allah is capable of all things. Power (qudrah) is the ability to choose and execute; unlike blind force, it does not imply necessity or compulsion. Allah does whatever He wills, and nothing can incapacitate Him.\n\nThese verses lay bare the indecision and hypocrisy of the hypocrites. They appear as though they want to benefit from Islam, but at the moment of danger, they flee. Yet true salvation lies in having faith in Allah and submitting to Him. A human being can be protected from Allah's punishment only by serving Him. Plugging one's ears after the lightning has struck is of no use; precautions must be taken beforehand.\n\nAfter explaining the classes of people and their capacities for guidance at the beginning of Surah al-Baqarah, Allah addresses all of mankind in the 21st verse and issues His first command: Worship your Lord, Who created you and those before you, that you may be protected. This command emphasizes the foundations of tawhid (divine oneness) and worship, which are the bedrock of Islam. Worship is a requirement of creation and an expression of gratitude; however, without Allah's grace, protection cannot be guaranteed—it can only be hoped for.\n\nIn the 22nd verse, Allah's blessings are enumerated: the earth as a couch, the sky as a canopy, rain, and provision. All of these blessings are manifestations of Allah's mercy. While people know all this, they must not set up rivals to Allah, nor accept partners alongside Him. The word 'andad' shows that everything equated with Allah is in truth fabricated and false.\n\nIn the 23rd verse, a challenge is issued to those who doubt: If you are in doubt that the Quran has come from Allah, produce a surah like it. This is the 'tahaddi' (challenge), which is the greatest miracle of the Prophet's prophethood. The peerless eloquence and content of the Quran constitute a miracle such that no human being could ever produce its like, and this fact has been established by experience across the centuries.\n\nIn the 24th verse, the outcome of this challenge is declared: If you cannot produce it—and you never will—then beware of the Fire of Hell whose fuel is men and stones. This fire has been prepared for the disbelievers. The word 'hijarah' mentioned in the verse may refer to idols as well as substances such as coal. This demonstrates the scientific aspect of the Quran.\n\nIn the 25th verse, glad tidings are given to those who believe and perform righteous deeds: for them are gardens beneath which rivers flow. There, they will say that the fruits offered to them resemble those of the world, but in truth they are far superior. The blessings in Paradise are the recompense for righteous deeds performed in this world, and they are eternal. Addressed to the Prophet, it is commanded that he proclaim this glad tiding.\n\nWhile describing the blessings of Paradise, pure spouses are mentioned. In these spouses, none of the worldly impurities exist; neither physical impurities such as menstruation and postpartum bleeding, nor moral defects such as immorality and discord are to be found. The Islamic land in this world should also be like such a paradise, yet the essential difference is this: the worldly homeland is transient and can be lost, whereas the Paradise of the Hereafter is eternal, and those who enter it will never depart. All glad tidings and bliss reside in this eternity.\n\nSome object to the mention of provision and women in these glad tidings; however, the pleasures of the Hereafter are not identical to those of this world, but vastly superior. The use of parables—that is, examples—in the Quran is also full of wisdom. Allah does not shy away from presenting a mosquito as an example. Those who believe know that these parables are the truth from their Lord; those who disbelieve mock, saying, 'What did Allah intend by this example?' Allah misguides many by these examples and guides many aright; yet those He misguides are only the defiantly disobedient (fasiqun).\n\nThe defiantly disobedient (fasiqun) are those who break Allah's covenant, sever kinship ties, and spread corruption on earth. In the lexicon, fisq means 'to depart' or 'to step out'; in religious terminology, it denotes departing from obedience to Allah by committing major sins. Fisq has three degrees: committing sins occasionally, committing them persistently, and committing them without regarding the sin as abhorrent. This third degree is disbelief (kufr). The defiantly disobedient in this verse are mentioned with their straying that amounts to disbelief.\n\nHow can you be ungrateful to Allah? When you were dead, He brought you to life; then He will cause you to die, then He will bring you to life again, and finally to Him you will be returned. In these two verses, the purpose of life, the worth of man, and Allah's grace are summarized. Whatever is on the earth was created entirely for you. Then Allah directed Himself to the heaven and fashioned them into seven heavens. In the face of these blessings, what a grave error disbelief and denial are!\n\nAllah created everything on the earth for human beings to benefit from and made them inherently permissible. However, the lives, honor, and property of people are not permissible to one another; violating them is a major sin. Humans were created not for one another, but to worship Allah. For this reason, a person cannot even dispose of his own life as he pleases. The legal maxim in Islamic jurisprudence (fiqh) that 'the default status of things is permissibility' is derived from this verse.\n\nAllah did not create man merely as a being who would live on the earth and benefit from the things upon it. He also allotted man a share from the heavens, and even made alterations across the seven heavens for this purpose. For this reason, man is not a creature belonging solely to the earth; if he feels constricted on earth, he is permitted to benefit from the heavens. For this, his soul must first experience celestial feelings and come to know Allah. Those who drown in earthly lusts and animalistic baseness, however, are deprived of this elevation.\n\nThe mention in the Quran of the ordering of the heavens after the creation of the earth is a sequence in terms of narrative order, not in terms of time. Human perception takes man as its center; it first recognizes the earth that is close, and then the heaven. For this reason, this sequence is followed in the verses. There are two main views regarding the 'seven heavens': One orders the seven layers of heaven from an astronomical perspective starting from the earth up to the planets; the other states that the physical heaven is the first heaven, beyond which lie six more spiritual heavens. These spiritual heavens pertain to the angels and divine commands.\n\nAfter these explanations, Allah informs the angels that He will create a vicegerent (khalifah) on the earth. 'Khalifah' means a deputy who succeeds someone and exercises authority on their behalf. The angels ask, 'Will You place therein one who will cause corruption and shed blood?' This question is not an objection, but an inquiry to understand the underlying wisdom. Furthermore, the angels recall that they glorify and sanctify Allah. Allah, however, replies, 'Indeed, I know that which you do not know.'\n\nThereafter, Allah teaches Adam all the names, shows them to the angels, and says, 'Tell Me the names of these, if you are truthful.' The angels say, 'Exalted are You; we have no knowledge except what You have taught us.' When Adam informs them of their names, Allah reminds the angels that He knows the unseen. Then, the angels are commanded to prostrate before Adam; all of them prostrate except Iblis. Iblis behaves arrogantly and disbelieves. Adam and his wife are settled in Paradise, yet Satan deceives them, causing their expulsion from Paradise. Allah opens the gate of repentance and accepts Adam's repentance. Humanity descends to earth in mutual enmity, and those who follow the guidance that comes from Allah attain salvation.\n\nThis narrative encompasses many beginnings: the beginning of human reproduction, religion, knowledge, language, duty and brotherhood, and law. It is emphasized that there is no original sin in human nature, and that sin and enmity occur subsequently through external factors. It recounts Satan's enmity toward man, and that man can struggle against this enmity by embracing and remaining true to his own innate disposition. The importance of repentance and man's high potential are recalled. Man was created as a product of the earth and the heavens, indeed as a grand microcosm; the means for all his happiness have been prepared. Man must now shake himself free from this base life, remember the blessings, attain the heights of his soul, and prepare for the Hereafter.\n\nThere are important explanations from Elmalılı's tafsir regarding the duties of the angels and their roles in the realm of existence. The angels, who express the diffusion of Allah's act of creation from unity into multiplicity, are messengers who convey divine commands, as well as beings who actively bring strength and assistance. No event in the universe occurs without an angel; for this reason, not even a drop of rain falls without an angel. Angels should be conceived of as constructive principles.\n\nPhilosophers and theologians (mutakallimun) have expressed different views regarding the nature of angels. According to the first view, angels are subtle bodies pertaining to the ether, possessing the power to change shape. The second view states that they are immaterial substances, resembling the human soul yet being more perfect. In the hadiths narrated from the Prophet (peace be upon him), the multitude of angels and the vast manifestations of Allah's power are described; so much so that up to the seventh heaven, each layer contains many times more angels than the previous ones.\n\nWhen Allah informed the angels that He would create a vicegerent on earth, the angels asked: 'Will You place therein one who will cause corruption and shed blood?' They expressed that they glorify and sanctify Allah. The Lord's response was: 'Indeed, I know that which you do not know.' This response indicates that there are aspects regarding the wisdom and reasons for the vicegerency (khilafah) of which the angels are unaware.\n\nAllah taught Adam all the names. There are various interpretations regarding what these names were. According to some, they are the names of all beings (human, animal, mountain, sea, etc.); according to others, the names of the angels; and according to still others, the names of Adam's descendants. Adam's learning of the names demonstrates how vital language and the attribute of speech (kalam) are for the vicegerency. The most prominent characteristic distinguishing man from other beings is this linguistic faculty.\n\nWhen the angels were unable to know the names taught to Adam, they admitted their inability and said, 'Subhanaka, we have no knowledge except what You have taught us.' Thereupon, Allah commanded Adam to inform them of those names, and Adam recited the names one by one. This demonstrated that Adam was worthy of the vicegerency and that the attributes of knowledge and speech were decisive in this regard.\n\nThe commentary also dwells on the origin of the name 'Adam'. Some scholars have stated that this name is not Arabic, but rather means 'earth' or 'soil' in Hebrew or Syriac. In Arabic, it is stated to be a diptote (ghayr munsarif) because it is used as a proper noun. These explanations emphasize the significance of Adam's personality and his place in human history.\n\nElmalılı explains the linguistic origin of the name Adam. He notes that the word Adam is used as a common noun in Arabic, and that it also appears in similar forms in Hebrew and Syriac. It is also related in some sources that Adam was called by different names in different languages. The most accurate view is that the name Adam is a word belonging to the primordial language of humanity.\n\nAllah commands the angels to prostrate before Adam. The angels immediately prostrate, with the exception of Iblis. Iblis, however, displays arrogance and disobeys the command. In reality, Iblis is of the jinn and among the disbelievers. His disbelief is not merely disobedience, but at the same time a disdain for Allah's command and a presumption of his own superiority.\n\nProstration (sajdah) is the highest degree of humility and reverence. The angels' prostration to Adam was not an act of worship toward Adam, but obedience to Allah's command and an act of service to the station of vicegerency. Through this prostration, the angels were tasked with assisting Adam. Human beings, as the children of Adam, have undertaken this very same duty of vicegerency.\n\nAllah commands Adam and his wife to dwell in Paradise and eat and drink freely therein as they please, but not to approach a specific tree. This prohibition delineates man's boundaries. What the tree was is not explained in the Quran; there are reports that it was wheat, grape, or fig. What is important is that this prohibition was instituted as a test and for servitude.\n\nSatan causes them to be expelled from Paradise by deceiving them into eating from this tree. Thereupon, Allah informs them to descend to the earth and that they will live as enemies to one another. Yet, a temporary enjoyment on earth is also ordained. This descent marks the beginning of human history.\n\nAdam realizes his mistake and repents by receiving certain words from his Lord. Allah accepts his repentance and shows His mercy. Repentance (tawbah) means turning back from the state of sin to one's primordial nature (fitrah). Allah is the Accepter of Repentance, the Merciful.\n\nAllah does not revoke the command to descend to the earth, but He promises to send guidance from Himself. Whoever follows this guidance, there shall be no fear upon them, nor shall they grieve. And whoever denies Allah's verses, they will abide in the Fire eternally. This is the beginning of humanity's journey of vicegerency (khilafah).\n\nThe address at the end of the story of Adam encompasses all of humanity. Adam and Eve, along with their offspring, represent an entire genus. The descent to the earth took place in multiplicity, and since we existed in the loins of Adam as an idea, we are included in this address. Human beings are fundamentally brethren, and this story emphasizes the common origin of humanity.\n\nIn this context, Elmalılı also touches upon the subject of creation and evolution. He maintains that the theory of evolution has not been scientifically proven, and that humans were created directly by Allah, not from apes. All humans are descendants of Adam and are brethren. The exact time of Adam's descent to the earth is not certain; it may have occurred hundreds of thousands of years ago. These views are the explanations presented in Elmalılı's tafsir.\n\nFollowing this story, Almighty Allah directs a specific address to the Children of Israel, who are the People of the Book. In verse 40, it is stated: \"O Children of Israel, remember My favor which I bestowed upon you, and fulfill your covenant with Me so that I will fulfill My covenant with you, and fear only Me.\" This favor is the scripture and prophethood given to them, as well as believing in the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him), who was heralded in the Torah. Their covenant was to follow the final prophet who was to be sent.\n\nIn verse 41, they are commanded to believe in the Quran which confirms the Torah in their possession, not to be the first to disbelieve in it, and not to exchange Allah's verses for a small worldly gain. In verse 42, they are forbidden from mixing truth with falsehood and concealing it, and from knowingly confounding truth with falsehood. This verse prohibits fraud in knowledge and action, distortion (tahrif), and presenting falsehood as truth.\n\nIn verse 43, they are commanded to establish prayer, give zakah, and bow with those who bow (the Muslim congregation). Ruku (bowing) is an act of worship specific to Islamic prayer, pointing to the importance of the congregation. In verse 44, they are rebuked for commanding people to do good while forgetting themselves. This addresses scholars and leaders in particular; counseling others while not practicing it oneself is a great contradiction. In a hadith narrated in al-Bukhari and Muslim, it is reported that on the Day of Resurrection, such a person will be turned around in Hellfire.\n\nIn verse 45, it is commanded to seek help from Allah through patience and prayer. Patience is the key to every distress, while prayer provides spiritual and physical discipline. Fasting also accustoms one to patience. These acts of worship deliver man from hardship and serve as a means to divine assistance. Patience and prayer protect a person from evils such as lying, deception, and concealing the truth.\n\nPatience and prayer are the ways of seeking Allah's help. This is not difficult except for those who possess humility (khushu')—that is, the reverent who know that they will meet their Lord and return to Him. They possess this awareness through certainty (yaqin) or preponderant conviction, and through this, patience and prayer bring them delight.\n\nO Children of Israel! Remember My favor that I bestowed upon you and that I favored you over the worlds. And beware of a Day when no soul will avail another soul at all, nor will intercession be accepted from it, nor will a ransom be taken, nor will they be helped. This warning is directed especially at the disbelievers and emphasizes that intercession is invalid without Allah's permission.\n\nThe first favor: We delivered you from the people of Pharaoh. They were subjecting you to the worst of torment, slaughtering your sons and letting your women live. In that was a tremendous trial from your Lord. The expression 'people of Pharaoh' indicates that the primary responsibility for the oppression belonged to those who followed him.\n\nThe second favor: We parted the sea and delivered you, and drowned the people of Pharaoh in the water. The third favor: We made an appointment with Moses (Musa) for forty nights, yet you took the calf for worship in his absence and became wrongdoers. Then We pardoned you, so that you might give thanks.\n\nThe fourth favor: We gave Moses the scripture and the criterion (furqan). The fifth favor: Moses said to you, 'Slay your selves'; this was better for your repentance, and Allah accepted your repentance. Indeed, He is the Accepter of Repentance, the Merciful. This event describes the eradication of the corruption of the calf and the application of the criterion.\n\nThe sixth favor: You said, 'We will never believe you until we see Allah openly,' so the thunderbolt struck you while you were looking on. Then We revived you after your death so that you might give thanks.\n\nIn this section, it is recounted that the affliction of the thunderbolt befell the Children of Israel when they demanded to see Allah openly from Prophet Moses, after which they were revived. Although this incident occurred not to the entire people but to the chosen seventy men, it was attributed to the whole people because responsibility was collective. This demonstrates that in divine law, in matters such as communal obligations (fard al-kifayah), the community is more important than the individual. This attitude of refusing to believe without seeing Allah is a great disbelief and ingratitude. This incident contains an important lesson regarding the states of ascent and decline of human beings.\n\nAmong the favors recalled thereafter are the shading of clouds over them in the wilderness of Tih and the sending down of manna and quails. Although they were told, 'Eat of the good provisions,' they showed ingratitude by hoarding and storing them up, and for this reason the blessing ceased. Later, the command was given: 'Enter this city, enter through the gate prostrating and say, \"hittah\" (forgive our sins), and We will forgive your transgressions.' However, the wrongdoers altered the word, saying another word instead of 'hittah,' and because of this, a foul torment was sent down upon them.\n\nOne of these favors was the gushing forth of twelve springs when Moses struck the rock with his staff. This miracle ensured that water was provided separately to the twelve tribes of the Children of Israel. The incident demonstrates the importance of acting in accordance with the divine command following supplication. Allah creates means, and for this reason, when a physical effort such as striking the rock with a staff was combined with spiritual supplication, the miracle occurred. From this, it is understood that one must supplicate to Allah from the heart and resort to the means that are inspired.\n\nLater, the Children of Israel stated that they were tired of a single kind of food and demanded vegetables, cucumbers, garlic, lentils, and onions. Moses said to them, 'Do you wish to exchange what is superior for what is inferior? Go down to a town, and you will find what you want.' This request of theirs was rooted in a vile desire, such as longing for slavery in Egypt. Because of this, the stamp of humiliation and misery was struck upon them, and they incurred the wrath of Allah. The reason for this was that they disbelieved in the verses of Allah and killed the prophets unjustly.\n\nLastly, it is stated that whoever among the believers, the Jews, the Christians, and the Sabians truly believes in Allah and the Last Day and works righteousness, they will have their reward with their Lord, and there will be no fear upon them, nor will they grieve. Here, it is emphasized that after Prophet Muhammad was sent as a prophet, true faith also encompasses believing in him. Since even those who believed in previous prophets were obligated to believe in the coming prophet, this verse indicates that they, too, must believe in the final prophet.\n\nThe Quran divides the concept of faith (iman) into outward and true. The prophethood of Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) is historically self-evident; denying it is a contradiction. In the verse, it is stated that whoever truly believes in Allah and the Last Day and works righteousness—including Jews, Christians, and Sabians—there shall be no fear upon them, nor shall they grieve. Although outward faith is sufficient for the formation of the Islamic community, true salvation is tied to complete faith and righteous deeds.\n\nThe word Yahudi (Jew) is derived in Arabic from repenting or from the name Judah. Nasara (Christians) traces back to Nazareth, the hometown of Jesus, or to tanasur, meaning mutual assistance. Sabi'in (Sabians), on the other hand, means those who change their religion, referring to groups that worship angels or stars.\n\nA covenant was taken from the Children of Israel to hold firmly to the scripture by raising Mount Tur above them. Yet they turned back on their word. Those among them who violated the Sabbath were struck by Allah's wrath and transformed into apes. This incident is recounted as a lesson and an admonition for past and future generations. Commentators are divided into two views on whether this transformation was physical or spiritual.\n\nMoses informed his people that Allah commanded them to slaughter a cow. Making a mockery of this, his people repeatedly questioned the cow's attributes. Allah stated that it was a cow neither old nor young, bright yellow, unyoked to the plow, and unblemished. Eventually, they found and slaughtered it, though they had almost failed to do so.\n\nThe Children of Israel had killed a man and cast the blame upon one another. Allah commanded them to strike the dead man with a piece of the slaughtered cow; the dead man came back to life and revealed his killer. Afterward, their hearts hardened, becoming like stone or even harder; yet from stones rivers gush forth, and some fall down out of the fear of Allah. Allah is not unaware of what they do. This story is a miracle demonstrating that resurrection after death is possible.\n\nAddressing the Children of Israel, Allah informs that despite all these miracles, their hearts hardened, becoming like stone or even harder. Yet from some stones rivers gush forth, some split open and water seeps out, and some fall down out of the fear of Allah. But their hearts remain utterly unaffected by revelation. Allah is not unaware of what they do.\n\nThe believers hoped that these hard-hearted people would come to faith. In truth, a faction of them would hear the Word of Allah and knowingly distort it after having understood it. When they met the believers, they would say, 'We believe,' but when they were alone among themselves, they would reproach one another, saying: 'Do you disclose to them the truths that Allah has revealed to you so that they may use them as an argument against you? Have you no sense?' Do they not know that Allah knows what they conceal and what they disclose?\n\nA portion of the Children of Israel are unlettered; they do not know the scripture, indulging only in wishful thinking and living in conjecture. Woe, then, to those who write the book with their own hands and then say, 'This is from Allah,' to fabricate a lie for a petty worldly gain. Woe to them for what their hands have written, and woe to them for the guilt they have earned!\n\nThe Jews said, 'The Fire will not touch us except for a few numbered days.' Elmalılı transmits various narrations regarding these numbered days: such as forty days, seven days, or seven thousand years. They utter against Allah things of which they have no knowledge. In truth, whoever commits an evil deed and is encompassed by their sin, they are the companions of the Fire, abiding therein eternally. As for those who believe and perform righteous deeds, they are the companions of Paradise, abiding therein eternally. This is the definitive promise of Allah.\n\nRemember when We took a covenant (mithaq) from the Children of Israel: You shall not worship anyone except Allah; you shall show kindness to parents, relatives, orphans, and the poor; speak good words to people; establish prayer; and give zakah. Then you turned away, except for a few among you. And remember when We took another covenant: You shall not shed each other's blood, nor expel one another from your homes. You affirmed this and you were witnesses. Yet you are the very ones who kill yourselves, expel a faction of your own from their homes, and support one another against them in enmity and wrongdoing; but if they come to you as captives, you ransom them, even though expelling them was forbidden to you. Do you, then, believe in part of the scripture and disbelieve in part? The recompense for this is nothing but disgrace in this worldly life, and on the Day of Resurrection, the most severe punishment. Allah is not unaware of what you do.\n\nThese are the ones who have bought the worldly life at the price of the Hereafter. For this reason, their punishment will not be lightened, nor will they be helped. And We certainly gave Moses the Scripture, followed him up with messengers, gave Jesus, the son of Mary, clear signs, and supported him with the Holy Spirit (Ruh al-Qudus). Will you, then, whenever a messenger comes to you with an order that your souls do not desire, act arrogantly, rejecting some and killing others?\n\nBayyinah means an evidence that is clear in itself and leaves no need for any other explanation. The miracles of the prophets are evidence of this kind. The bayyinat of Jesus are his miracles that clearly demonstrate his prophethood. The name 'Isa (Jesus) derives from the Syriac 'Isho'; Maryam (Mary), on the other hand, means 'servant' in Syriac.\n\nThe explicit mention of Jesus among the prophets after Moses is because the religion of Jesus abrogated certain rulings in the Torah and constituted a distinct religion.\n\nThe concept of spirit (ruh) is addressed from three primary perspectives: spirit as the origin of motion, spirit as the source of life, and spirit as the source of perception. Motion, life, and perception are the essential attributes of the spirit. The spirit is an entity opposed to matter, imparting life, motion, and perception to living beings.\n\nCommentators have put forward different views regarding the Holy Spirit (Ruh al-Qudus): the Spirit of Allah, the Greatest Name of Allah, the Gospel, or Gabriel. According to the most sound view, the Holy Spirit is Gabriel. Jesus was specifically supported with this spirit because Gabriel accompanied him ever since his mother's pregnancy.\n\nThe Jews mocked the invitation of our Prophet (peace be upon him) by saying, 'Our hearts are wrapped.' Allah cursed them for their disbelief. Previously, however, they had been awaiting the arrival of a prophet heralded in the Torah and praying for victory through him against the polytheists. Yet when the prophet they were waiting for arrived, they denied him out of envy and selfishness. For this reason, they incurred the wrath of Allah.\n\nWhile the Jews were waiting for the end-times prophet foretold in the Torah and asserting that they would prevail through him, they denied him out of envy when that prophet arrived. Through this denial, they both deserved the former wrath and incurred a new wrath upon themselves. They were also debased by rendering void the ruling of their own scripture.\n\nWhen it is said to them, 'Believe in what Allah has revealed,' they say, 'We believe only in what was revealed to us.' Yet the Quran confirms the Torah that is with them. They are asked: If you were truly believers, why did you slay the prophets of Allah before?\n\nMoses came to you with clear proofs, then after him you worshipped the calf. We took a covenant from you by raising Mount Tur above you, and you said, 'We heard and disobeyed.' How vile are the things your faith commands you to do, that you kill prophets and disbelieve in your scripture!\n\nSay: If the home of the Hereafter belongs to you and you are truthful in this claim, then wish for death. Yet they will never wish for it, because of the sins their hands have committed. You will find them the greediest of people for life; even greedier than the polytheists. The desire for a long life will not save them from punishment.\n\nThe meaning the Jews assign to the expression 'the abode of the Hereafter' is the ideal of establishing an earthly state. This belief of theirs is contradictory, because without believing in the true Hereafter, no sacrifice can be made for such a cause. Their fleeing from death demonstrates that these claims of theirs are false.\n\nAs for the hostility toward Gabriel, some Jews harbor enmity toward him, saying 'he brings war and violence.' Yet it is he who brought down the Quran. Allah declares that whoever is an enemy to Gabriel and Michael is a disbeliever, and that He is likewise their enemy.\n\nThe Jews harbored enmity by claiming that Allah had commanded Gabriel to deliver the prophethood to them, but that Gabriel had brought it to Muhammad (peace be upon him) instead. However, the verse informs that the true reason for this enmity is that the Quran was revealed into the heart of Muhammad (peace be upon him), and that this took place by Allah's permission. Furthermore, the Quran confirms the previous scriptures and is a source of guidance and glad tidings for the believers; therefore, harboring hostility toward Gabriel is hostility toward Allah and His scriptures.\n\nThe word \"Jibril\" is composed in Hebrew of \"jebr\" (servant) and \"il\" (God/Allah), meaning \"Abdullah\" (servant of Allah). Some commentators, however, give it the meaning of \"Allah's omnipotence (jabarut)\" (His majesty). In Arabic, it is recited with different variant readings (qira'at), such as Jabra'il, Jabre'il, Jabril, and Jibril. This name conveys the certainty and irresistibility of revelatory knowledge. Mentioning the angel in this manner also defines his duty: a source of knowledge possessing cognitive and practical necessity that no power can obstruct.\n\nIn the verse, it is proclaimed: \"Say: Whoever is an enemy to Gabriel—it is he who brought the Quran down upon your heart by Allah's permission.\" This is a warning; for Gabriel brings the Quran, which confirms the previous scriptures and provides guidance and glad tidings to the believers. Consequently, enmity toward Gabriel is regarded as enmity toward Allah, His scriptures, and His messengers. Allah, too, is an enemy to the disbelievers; their being granted respite is not a blessing, but an affliction. However, if they repent and believe, they will partake in guidance and glad tidings.\n\nThe expression \"upon your heart\" conveys that the revelation completely encompasses and envelops the heart. Indeed, when revelation descended, it left a heavy impact upon the Prophet (peace be upon him), to such an extent that he endured great hardship, even sweating on a cold day. This demonstrates that divine knowledge is necessary and definitive. Moreover, the expression \"upon your heart\" (ala qalbik) rather than \"upon you\" (alayk) emphasizes that the speech was uttered not by the Messenger of Allah, but by Allah Himself.\n\nIn verse 99, Allah declares that He has revealed clear verses, and that none will reject them except the defiantly disobedient (fasiq). In verse 100, it is stated that whenever they enter into a covenant, a party among them breaks it, and most of them do not believe. In verse 101, it is related that when a messenger came to them confirming the scripture they already possessed, a party of the People of the Book cast the Book of Allah behind their backs.\n\nIn verse 102, it is stated that they followed what the devils fabricated concerning the kingdom of Solomon. Solomon did not disbelieve, but the devils disbelieved, teaching people magic. They also conveyed to students what had been revealed in Babylon to the two angels named Harut and Marut; yet the angels would warn, \"We are only a test, so do not practice magic and become disbelievers.\" The magicians would learn from them that by which they cause separation between a man and his wife, yet they could not harm anyone with it except by Allah's permission. They acquired that which harmed them and brought them no share in the Hereafter. Had they only believed and been mindful of Allah, the reward from Allah would have been far better.\n\nLinguistically, magic (sihr) means a subtle matter whose cause is hidden. In the religious sense, it is presenting truth as falsehood and falsehood as truth, illusion, and deception. The types of magic include methods such as talismans, exerting influence through spiritual force, and enlisting the aid of the jinn. However, the core issue is the relationship between magic and disbelief (kufr); teaching and practicing magic alienates one from faith. The verse underscores that those who engage in magic have no share in the Hereafter.\n\nTypes of magic are divided into four: deceiving the eye (illusion/conjuring), sleight of hand performed with mechanical devices, magic produced with chemical substances, and captivating the heart (suggestion) along with calumny (gossip and discord). These are gathered under two fundamental principles: that which is sheer falsehood and deception, and that which is more or less the abuse of truth. Magic cannot be described as entirely unreal in every instance; some of it is mingled with reality. To indicate these two categories, the verse uses first the term \"magic\" and subsequently \"that which was revealed to Harut and Marut.\"\n\nIn the narrative of Harut and Marut, what was sent down to them was not actually magic, but knowledge susceptible to misuse. When teaching, they would caution, \"We are but a trial, so do not fall into disbelief.\" The devils, however, taught it as magic and led people into disbelief. Elmalılı takes the particle \"ma\" in the verse not as a negative particle, but as a relative pronoun (mawsul), and notes that this aligns with the context of the verse. This knowledge was also imparted to the people of Babylon by way of inspiration, but was transformed into magic through its misuse.\n\nThe Babylonians fell into disbelief by attributing the celestial and terrestrial forces they learned from Harut and Marut to the stars. Later, the devils instigated discord against the rule of Solomon (peace be upon him) using this knowledge. The Jews, too, learned these magical practices and did things to cause separation between a husband and his wife. Yet they can cause no harm except by Allah's permission; true causal efficacy belongs to Allah alone. Those who are deceived by magic have no share in the Hereafter.\n\nAddressing the believers: Do not say 'Ra'ina', but say 'Unzurna' and listen attentively. The Jews used this word as an insult. Furthermore, the expression 'ra'ina' carries a connotation of uncouthness and gives the false impression of mutual regard. Yet proper etiquette toward the prophet is not to watch over him, but to submit to his oversight. Believers must distance themselves from animalistic conceptions and embrace humane stewardship. This verse teaches the foundation of leadership (imamah) and social order.\n\nThe disbelievers do not wish for any good to be sent down to you from your Lord. Allah singles out for His mercy whom He wills. As for the abrogation (naskh) of verses, Allah brings what is better or similar to them. Abrogation is not contrary to Allah's power; to Him belongs the dominion of the heavens and the earth. Do not interrogate your messenger as Moses was questioned before. Whoever exchanges faith for disbelief has strayed from the straight path.\n\nIn verse 106 of Surah al-Baqarah, it is declared that Allah may repeal the ruling of certain verses and bring what is better or similar in their place. This is termed abrogation (naskh). Naskh is the subsequent introduction of another evidence in place of a legal ruling (shar'i ruling). This modification signifies that, according to divine knowledge, the term of the ruling has ended; it does not denote any deficiency in Allah's knowledge or power. Abrogation does not occur in eternal matters such as the tenets of faith, but in temporary rulings such as commands and prohibitions. There are also textual variant readings (qira'at) of the verse: in some readings it is recited as 'nunsikh' (We abrogate), while in others as 'nunsi' (We cause to be forgotten). These variants enrich the meaning.\n\nIn verse 107, it is emphasized that Allah has power over all things and that to Him belongs the dominion of the heavens and the earth. The Possessor of this infinite power may alter whichever ruling He wills, whenever He wills. In verse 108, believers are reminded not to make extraordinary demands of their messenger, as was demanded of Moses (peace be upon him). Whoever exchanges faith for disbelief has strayed from the right path.\n\nVerse 109 conveys that many of the People of the Book wish to turn the believers back from their faith out of envy, even after the truth has become clearly evident to them. The occasion of revelation (sabab al-nuzul) of this verse was that after the Battle of Uhud, certain Jews provoked Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman and Ammar ibn Yasir, attempting to turn them away from their religion. The believers are commanded to forgive and overlook these individuals until Allah's command comes.\n\nIn verse 110, establishing prayer properly and giving zakah are commanded. Moreover, whatever good a person sends forth for their own soul will find its reward with Allah. Allah sees all that is done, and no good deed will go unrewarded.\n\nIn verse 111, it is reported that the Jews and Christians claim that no one will enter Paradise except themselves. This is merely their wishful thinking. They are demanded to produce proof to substantiate this claim. There is also a variant in the recitation (qira'ah) of the verse read as 'umniyyah', which indicates that they are merely engaged in vain desires.\n\nVerse 112 explains the true path to entering Paradise: Whoever submits their face completely to Allah while being a doer of good, their reward is with their Lord. There will be no fear concerning them, nor will they grieve. Here, the expression 'face' represents the entirety of a person's being. Islam signifies submission and peace (salamah), while ihsan is to worship Allah as though one sees Him. These two concepts constitute the essence of the religion.\n\nIn verse 113, it is reported that the Jews say, 'The Christians have nothing to stand on,' and the Christians say, 'The Jews have nothing to stand on.' Yet both groups read the scripture. The occasion of revelation for this verse was a dispute between the Christians of Najran and the Jews of Medina. Both sides fundamentally reject one another. Allah will judge between them on the Day of Resurrection. This verse warns believers against such disputes and directs them toward sincerity (ikhlas).\n\nWhile constantly reading the same scripture, the Jews and Christians said to one another, \"They have nothing to stand on.\" Those who possess no knowledge said the same thing. Muslims must not act like this; they should not categorically dismiss others, but rather base their words on evidence and debate in the best manner. Allah will judge their disputes on the Day of Resurrection.\n\nIn verse 114, it is stated that none is more unjust than those who prevent the mention of Allah's name in His mosques and strive for their ruin. Such wrongdoers should not enter them except in fear. For them is disgrace in this world, and for them is a great punishment in the Hereafter. The occasion of revelation of the verse relates to the destruction of Bayt al-Maqdis (the Holy House in Jerusalem); the Romans and Byzantine Christians perpetrated this injustice, and it later passed into the hands of the Muslims. This serves as an admonition for Muslims today as well.\n\nIn verse 115, it is declared that both the east and the west belong to Allah; so wherever you turn, there is the face of Allah. Allah's mercy is all-encompassing, and He knows all things. A mosque is not strictly required for prayer; in case of necessity, any direction can serve as the qiblah. This verse also points to the change of the qiblah and provides ease for Muslims.\n\nIn verses 116–118, those who say that Allah has taken a child are condemned. The Jews said, \"Ezra (Uzayr) is the son of Allah,\" the Christians said, \"The Messiah is the son of Allah,\" and the polytheists said, \"The angels are the daughters of Allah.\" Allah is the Originator of the heavens and the earth; when He decrees a matter, He merely says to it, \"Be!\" and it is. Those without knowledge said, \"Why does Allah not speak to us, or why does a sign not come to us?\" Yet those before them had said the same thing. Allah has made the signs clear for people who possess certainty.\n\nIn verses 119–121, it is stated that the Prophet was sent with the truth as a bearer of glad tidings and a warner, and that he is not responsible for the companions of the Hellfire. The Jews and the Christians will never be pleased until the Prophet follows their religion (millah). The guidance of Allah is the only true guidance. If one were to follow their desires after the knowledge that has come through revelation, one would find neither protector nor helper against Allah. Those who recite the Book as it ought to be recited believe in it; those who disbelieve in it are the losers.\n\nWhen explaining the concept of \"millah\" (religious path/community), Elmalılı Hamdi Yazır defines it as the path around which a community gathers and upon which it walks. This path consists of the principles and course being followed. Millah is not the society itself, but the prevailing principles to which that society adheres. Judaism and Christianity are each a millah, yet they are religions that have deviated from the true path and been corrupted by following whims and desires. Allah strictly warned His Prophet against following such false paths.\n\nIn verse 121, mention is made of the People of the Book. Those who recite the Book given by Allah as it ought to be recited read it distinctly, with reverence, and by comprehending its meaning. These are the ones who truly believe in the scripture and become genuine People of the Book. Whoever disbelieves in the scripture or alters it is among the losers. This verse encompasses figures like Abdullah ibn Salam, who recited the Torah properly and subsequently embraced Islam, as well as the forty individuals who arrived from Abyssinia.\n\nIn verses 122–123, the Children of Israel are addressed: they are asked to remember the blessings and favor bestowed upon them. Furthermore, they are commanded to guard against a Day when no soul will avail another, no ransom will be accepted, and no intercession will benefit. This is a warning issued for the third time.\n\nIn verse 124, the trial of Abraham (Ibrahim) is recounted. His Lord tested him with certain commands, and when Abraham fulfilled them, Allah declared that He would make him a leader (imam) for mankind. Abraham pleaded that leaders be made from his offspring as well, but Allah stated that His covenant does not include the wrongdoers. This verse serves as evidence that an oppressor is not worthy of leadership and that anyone lacking justice must be removed from this station. It is emphasized that due to the transgressions of the Children of Israel, leadership departed from them and passed to the lineage of Ishmael (Ismail).\n\nIn verse 125, it is recalled that the Kaaba was made a place of return (and reward) and security for mankind. It is commanded that the Station of Abraham (Maqam Ibrahim) be taken as a place of prayer, and that the Kaaba be kept pure for those who circumambulate, those who stay in devotion (i'tikaf), and those who bow and prostrate in prayer. This also hints at the change of the qiblah. The covenant entrusted to Abraham and Ishmael was to attend with utmost care to the purity of this House.\n\nVerse 126 contains the supplication of Abraham. He asked that Mecca be made a secure city and that its people who believe be provided with fruits. Allah, however, made known that He would also provide for the disbelievers in this world, but would drive them to the torment of the Fire in the Hereafter. This shows that even prophets can err in their independent judgment (ijtihad), but Allah corrects their mistakes. Sustenance, unlike spiritual leadership (imamah), is not a blessing restricted solely to believers.\n\nAs Abraham and his son Ishmael were raising the foundations of the Kaaba, they prayed: 'Our Lord, accept this service from us. Indeed, You are the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing.' They also beseeched: 'Make us submissive (Muslim) to You, and from our descendants make a community submissive to You; show us our rites of worship, and accept our repentance. Indeed, You are the Accepter of Repentance, the Merciful.'\n\nAbraham and Ishmael prayed, 'Our Lord, send among our descendants a messenger who will recite to them Your verses, teach them the Book and wisdom, and purify them. Indeed, You are the Almighty, the All-Wise.' This supplication points to the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him), as no other prophet came from the lineage of Ishmael. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: 'I am the supplication of my father Abraham, the glad tidings of my brother Jesus, and the vision of my mother.'\n\nWhoever turns away from the religion of Abraham is only one who degrades himself into debasement. According to narration, Abdullah ibn Salam invited his nephews to Islam; one of them believed, while the other did not. Allah made known that He chose Abraham and that he was His friend. Abraham submitted completely to his Lord and turned to Him as an upright monotheist (hanif).\n\nAbraham and Jacob enjoined their sons: 'Allah has chosen this religion for you, so do not die except as Muslims.' On his deathbed, Jacob asked his sons: 'What will you worship after me?' They replied: 'We will worship your God and the God of your fathers, Abraham, Ishmael, and Isaac—one God—and to Him we submit.'\n\nThe fact that Jacob's sons said 'our fathers Abraham, Ishmael, and Isaac' indicates that the sons of Ishmael were also their brothers. In the Torah, it was stated through the tongue of Moses: 'I will send a prophet from among your brothers.' This prophet is the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him), who descends from the line of Ishmael. There are many glad tidings concerning him in both the Torah and the Gospel.\n\nIn the Torah, it was foretold that Ishmael's hand would be above all and that someone would appear from Mount Paran; in the Psalms (Zabur), that a prophet bearing a divine law would be sent; and in the Gospel, that the Paraclete (Faraqlit) would come. All of these verses confirm the prophethood of the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him). After these proofs, Allah declared: 'That was a community that has passed away. For them is what they earned, and for you is what you have earned. And you will not be questioned about what they used to do.'\n\nThese verses emphasize that past communities bear their own responsibility, while we are accountable for our own deeds. Prophets like Abraham and Jacob were a community; they have passed away. What they earned belongs to them, and what we have earned belongs to us. Boasting of lineage and ancestry is of no avail; everyone will be held to account for their own striving. Our Prophet (peace be upon him) also said to the Banu Hashim: 'Do not come to me merely with your lineage.'\n\nThe Jews and the Christians say, 'Be Jews or Christians, so that you may be guided.' Say to them: 'No, rather we follow the religion of Abraham, as an upright monotheist (hanif); he was not of the polytheists.' We believe in Allah, in what has been revealed to us, in what was revealed to Abraham, Ishmael, Isaac, Jacob, and the Descendants, in what was given to Moses and Jesus, and to all the prophets; we make no distinction between any of them, and to Allah we are submitted.\n\nIf they believe as you have believed, they will have found the right path; but if they turn away, they are in dissension. Allah is sufficient for you. Allah's dye (sibghah) is the best; and we worship Him. It is not the artificial dye that Christians call baptism, but innate faith (fitrah) that is the dye of Allah.\n\nDo you dispute with us concerning Allah, while He is our Lord and your Lord? For us are our deeds, and for you are your deeds, and we are sincerely devoted to Him. Or do you say that Abraham, Ishmael, Isaac, Jacob, and the Descendants were Jews or Christians? Say: 'Do you know better, or does Allah?' And who is more unjust than one who conceals a testimony from Allah? Allah is not unaware of what you do.\n\nIn these verses, there are several warnings: Do not boast of past ancestors; everyone is responsible for their own deeds. The abrogation of rulings (naskh) is possible; this is a foundation of the science of jurisprudence (fiqh). Do not blindly imitate predecessors; distinguish truth from falsehood. These principles are reiterated and reinforced in this section of Surah al-Baqarah.\n\nSome foolish people among mankind will say, 'What has turned them away from their qiblah that they were facing?' Say: 'To Allah belong the east and the west; He guides whom He wills to a straight path.' Thus We have made you a middle community (wasat) so that you may be witnesses over mankind and the Messenger may be a witness over you. Our changing the former qiblah was only to distinguish those who follow the Messenger from those who turn back on their heels; and indeed, it was burdensome except for those whom Allah has guided.\n\nWe see you turning your face toward the heaven; now We will surely turn you to a qiblah that will please you. Turn your face toward al-Masjid al-Haram; wherever you may be, turn your faces toward it. Those who were given the Scripture know that it is the truth from their Lord. Even if you were to bring them every kind of proof, they still would not follow your qiblah; nor should you follow their qiblah. Besides, they do not follow each other's qiblah either. If you were to follow their desires after the knowledge that has come to you, you would certainly be among the wrongdoers.\n\nIn these verses, while some scholars of recitation (qira'ah) read with the letter 'ya', others read it differently. In terms of meaning, Allah bears witness that Abraham and the other prophets were neither Jews nor Christians. O Muhammad, say to them: 'Do you know better, or does Allah?' Allah knows that they were upright monotheists (hanif) and Muslims. Yet you knowingly deny this truth. Who could be more unjust than one who conceals a truthful testimony? Allah has declared that the wrongdoers will not attain His covenant. Past communities have passed away; what they earned belongs to them, and what you have earned belongs to you. You are not responsible for what they used to do.\n\nCommentators of the Quran note that there are several important warnings in this verse: First, rather than boasting of the virtues of past ancestors, everyone will be called to account for their own deeds. Second, it is possible for rulings prescribed for past communities to change; indeed, rulings change with the changing of times. This is among the flexible principles of Islam, ensuring its endurance until the Day of Resurrection. Third, those who wish to walk the path of the prophets must not blindly imitate their predecessors, but must act by distinguishing truth from falsehood, for everyone is responsible for what they themselves have done.\n\nRegarding the change of the qiblah, some foolish people will say, 'What has turned them away from their qiblah?' Say to them: 'To Allah belong the east and the west. He guides whom He wills to a straight path.' Here lies an answer to the supplication in Surah al-Fatihah. This new way is a straight path (sirat al-mustaqim) that is more beautiful than the previous one and leads to safety and salvation. This guidance depends entirely on the will of Allah; the servant's striving comes only after this bestowal of grace. Nor is prophethood attained through personal effort.\n\nAllah has made you a middle community (wasat) so that you may be witnesses over mankind and the Prophet may be a witness over you. A witness is one who is just and speaks the truth. In order to become such an exemplary community, Muslims have been guided to a new straight path (sirat al-mustaqim) under the guidance of the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him). The consensus (ijma') of this community serves as evidence for the truth. However, this distinction depends on living in accordance with the Book and the Sunnah. Otherwise, they are doomed to become satellites of other nations.\n\nWhile our Prophet (peace be upon him) used to perform the prayer facing the Kaaba in Mecca, after the migration (hijrah) to Medina, he turned toward Jerusalem. Later, the command to turn back to the Kaaba arrived. Regarding this change, Allah stated that He saw his face turning toward the heaven and that He would turn him to a qiblah with which he would be pleased. Turn your face toward al-Masjid al-Haram now. Wherever you may be, turn toward that direction. The People of the Book know that this is a truth from their Lord. Allah is not unaware of what they do.\n\nO Muhammad, even if you were to bring the People of the Book every kind of proof, they would not follow your qiblah, nor will you follow their qiblah. They do not follow each other's qiblah either. If you were to follow their desires after the knowledge that has come to you, you would surely be among the wrongdoers. The scholars of the People of the Book recognize the Prophet just as they recognize their own sons; yet a group of them knowingly conceal the truth. The address in the third person (gha'ib) in this verse conveys the sense of an objective testimony.\n\nThe People of the Book recognized the final prophet as 'that prophet' due to the descriptions recounted in the Torah. Before the clear verses and miracles brought by the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him), their scholars had no doubt left; they knew him as conclusively as they knew their own children. However, this knowledge is not sufficient for faith. For faith, alongside knowledge in the heart, obedience and open affirmation are also required. Because they disbelieved despite knowing, they became among the disbelievers who knowingly concealed the truth.\n\nAddressing the Prophet, Allah states: 'The truth is from your Lord, so never be among the doubters.' This address also warns the ummah and particularly reinforces the matter of the qiblah. The qiblah is the primary physical manifestation ensuring the spiritual unity of an ummah. There cannot be an ummah without a qiblah. Because the Kaaba was the qiblah of Abraham, it is worthy of being the most comprehensive direction of unity. Even though the orientation of each community toward the Kaaba differs, they all gather at a single center.\n\nEvery community has a direction to which it turns. The believers, too, should race in good deeds by turning toward the Kaaba. The purpose of the qiblah is to embark upon a race for good deeds with orderly unity. Wherever you may be, Allah will bring you all together. He is capable of all things. Turn your faces toward the Sacred Mosque (al-Masjid al-Haram) in prayer; this command is the truth from your Lord. Allah is not unaware of what you do.\n\nThe command regarding the qiblah was repeated so that people would have no argument against you. The People of the Book and the polytheists are left unable to raise objections concerning the change of the qiblah. Yet the wrongdoers still speak out, but their words are invalid. Do not fear them, but fear Allah. This command is so that He may complete His favor upon you and that you may be guided. The completion of the favor is to die upon Islam and enter Paradise.\n\nAllah sent a messenger from among yourselves. He recites His verses to you, purifies you, teaches you the Book and wisdom, and teaches you what you did not know. This is a tremendous favor. Making the Kaaba the qiblah is also for the completion of this favor. For this, you have two duties: remember Me, and I will remember you; give thanks to Me, and do not be ungrateful. Remembrance (dhikr) occurs through the tongue, heart, and body. Gratitude (shukr) is also expressed through these means. The beginning of servitude is remembrance, and its culmination is gratitude.\n\nO you who believe! Seek help through patience and prayer. Allah is with those who are patient. Patience is the beginning of every success. Prayer, on the other hand, is the greatest strengthener of faith and a means of drawing near to Allah. Do not say of those who are killed in the cause of Allah, 'They are dead'; rather, they are alive, but you do not perceive it. We will surely test you with something of fear, hunger, and loss of wealth, lives, and crops. And give glad tidings to the patient.\n\nWhen an affliction strikes them, they say, 'To Allah we belong, and to Him we shall return.' Upon them are forgiveness and mercy from their Lord. And it is they who are rightly guided. Patience and prayer are the most important means of drawing divine assistance. Allah's being with the patient is a great honor He bestows upon them.\n\nBelievers, seek help from Allah through patience and prayer. Do not call those slain in the cause of Allah 'dead'; they are not dead, but alive. However, you cannot perceive their life. This is a spiritual life. As narrated from Hasan al-Basri, the provisions of the martyrs are presented to their souls. This verse is an indication that the soul remains conscious after death. The majority of the Companions and the Successors (tabi'un) hold this view. The specific mention of the martyrs here is to make known their exalted station. The life of the Hereafter, however, is broader; matters such as resurrection after death and the realm of the isthmus (barzakh) will be explained in due course.\n\nAllah will test you with something of fear, hunger, and a loss of wealth, lives, and crops. These tribulations are a taste in this world to lead toward the immense blessings of the Hereafter. Give glad tidings to the patient; in times of affliction, they say, 'Inna lillahi wa inna ilayhi raji'un' (Truly to Allah we belong, and truly to Him we shall return). These words mean surrendering one's wealth and life to Allah, and being content with whatever He wills to do. Moreover, they express faith that we will not perish through death but will return to Allah, as well as the hope of being well-pleased with Him. This station is referred to as 'al-nafs al-radiyah' (the content soul) and 'al-nafs al-mardiyyah' (the soul well-pleasing to Him).\n\nThe soul (nafs) rises successively through the levels of first 'ammarah' (inciting to evil), then 'lawwamah' (self-reproaching), then 'mutma'innah' (tranquil), then 'radiyah' (content), and finally 'mardiyyah' (well-pleasing to Allah). The address in Surah al-Fajr, 'O tranquil soul! Return to your Lord,' conveys this. The return is not spatial; Allah the Exalted is transcendent beyond space. This return is an acknowledgment of our soul coming to an end, an acknowledgment of enduring, or a state between annihilation and everlasting existence. In truth, our existence exists through Allah's knowledge, and we endure through His eternity. In the Hereafter, we will be resurrected in a new creation.\n\nSome philosophers viewed the Hereafter solely as the soul remaining immortal, but this is erroneous. The soul is a mystery from Allah's command, but our endurance is through Allah's eternity. The statement 'We belong to Allah, and to Him we shall return' is a reality not confined merely to the soul or the body, but beyond both. The Hereafter occurs through the resurrection after the life of the isthmus (barzakh). This return is an attainment of Allah's grace or His wrath, and it takes place through subsisting in Allah (baqa bi'llah). Hope and fear must rest upon this foundation.\n\nSafa and Marwah are among the symbols of Allah. There is no sin upon whoever performs Hajj or Umrah to walk (sa'y) between them. This verse was revealed because Muslims hesitated due to the presence of idols there during the pre-Islamic period of ignorance (Jahiliyyah). Our Prophet (peace be upon him) performed this circumambulation and commanded the sa'y. In the Hanafi school of law, this sa'y is regarded as obligatory (wajib). It is based on the commemoration of Hagar, the wife of Abraham (peace be upon him), running between Safa and Marwah while suffering from thirst. This demonstrates the importance of seeking refuge in Allah and exercising patience in moments of distress. Voluntary good deeds also carry immense reward; in a sacred hadith (hadith qudsi), it is stated that the believer draws near to Allah through voluntary deeds.\n\nThose who conceal the clear proofs and verses of guidance that Allah has sent down, after He has made them clear to the people, are cursed by Allah and cursed by all who can curse. Except those who repent, reform their condition, and make the truth clear; they will be forgiven. As for those who disbelieve in Our verses and die as disbelievers, they shall remain under an eternal curse, and their punishment will not be lightened. This ruling is general; it encompasses anyone who conceals a religious truth. Abu Hurayrah stated that he narrated hadiths relying upon these verses.\n\nIn this section, after attention is drawn once again to the story of Adam (peace be upon him), the condition of those who repent is described. Those who repented and reformed their condition made the truths known. This shows that repentance is tied to the reward of declaration and disclosure, which is the opposite of the sin of concealment. Every sin has a repentance specific to itself; concealing a manifest truth is disbelief, whereas faith is declaring the truth. In verse 161, it is stated that those who do not believe in the manifest proofs and guidance sent down by Allah, along with all the prophets and scriptures, the prophethood of Muhammad (peace be upon him), and the Quran, but persist in disbelief and die as disbelievers, are the cursed ones.\n\nIn verse 162, it is stated that the curse of Allah, the angels, and all humanity is upon them, that they will abide under the curse eternally, and that their punishment will not be lightened. Since all disbelief is the concealment of a truth, this verse encompasses everyone other than those excepted through repentance in the previous verse. Furthermore, this verse establishes a more general ruling of condemnation by also including those who, even if they articulated the truth with their tongues or pens, did not believe in their hearts and died in disbelief.\n\nIn verse 163, monotheism (tawhid) is emphasized: 'Your God is surely one God. There is no god but Him. He is the Most Compassionate (al-Rahman), the Most Merciful (al-Rahim).' This verse calls people to abandon disbelief and discord and enter the fold of monotheism. The oneness of Allah is unique in His essence and His attributes; there is no true god besides Him. The pronoun 'Huwa' is counted among the greatest names that indicate His essence, while the name 'Allah' signifies the totality of His essence and attributes. He is both al-Rahman and al-Rahim; all mercy belongs to Him.\n\nThe polytheists had demanded a physical miracle from the Prophet (peace be upon him), such as turning Mount Safa into gold. Thereupon, Allah Almighty revealed verse 164, declaring that in the creation of the heavens and the earth, the alternation of night and day, the ships that sail through the sea, the sending down of rain to revive the dead earth, the dispersing of living creatures, the shifting of the winds, and the clouds, there are many signs (ayat) for people of understanding. These signs are greater, more beneficial, and more enduring proofs than the miracle that was demanded.\n\nThe signs described in this verse are clear proofs for a people who use their reason. Intellect (aql) is a spiritual light that serves to understand things that cannot be perceived through the senses. Reasoning is moving from effect to cause or from cause to effect by grasping the law of causality between causes and effects. There are various kinds of intellect: reflective thought (fikr), intuition (hads), heard/learned intellect (aql-i masmu'), and sacred intellect (qudsi aql). The highest rank is the 'first intellect' (aql-i awwal), which is the light of the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him). The Quran encourages people to exercise their intellect, and through this path, it teaches reaching the existence, oneness, and mercy of Allah.\n\nIn verses 165–167, it is stated that among people are those who take equals besides Allah, loving them as they should love Allah, whereas the love of believers for Allah is stronger. On the Day of Resurrection, those who were followed will disown those who followed them, and all ties will be severed. The followers will say, 'If only we had another turn in the world, so that we might disown them as they have disowned us.' Allah will show them their deeds as regrets, and they will never emerge from the Fire. These verses explain that excessively loving things other than Allah is polytheism (shirk), resulting in eternal punishment.\n\nLove for Allah and love for the Prophet are the completion of faith. A believer loves Allah, and loves the Prophet for His sake. Mingling anyone other than Allah into the intention during prayer is disbelief (kufr). One only prays on behalf of the Prophet or righteous servants; one does not ask anything from them. Loving them as one loves Allah, offering sacrifices in their name, or invoking their names like 'Bismillah' is polytheism. Such superstitions emerge in ignorant eras when religious knowledge has weakened.\n\nThe unity of existence (wahdat al-wujud) requires understanding the distinction between saying 'There is no existent being other than Allah' and saying 'Every existent being is Allah.' The first means regarding the existence of the universe as a shadow and illusion and acknowledging that real existence belongs to Allah. The second means accepting multiplicity and attributing divinity to everything, which is polytheism. This is the false doctrine known as pantheism. The monotheism of Islam is the principle of 'There is no god but Allah'; it should not be understood as 'There is no existence other than Him.'\n\nCertain extremist groups, such as Wahhabism, have considered reverence for prophets and righteous servants to be polytheism. Yet loving and showing reverence for the sake of Allah is not polytheism. Visiting graves should be done in accordance with the Sunnah, and it should serve as a reminder of death. Praying for the deceased and wishing goodness for them is not loving them as one loves Allah. It is necessary to avoid extremism; neither neglecting them nor idolizing them is correct.\n\nThe believers' love for Allah is continuous; they turn to Allah under all circumstances. Polytheists, on the other hand, remember Allah in times of distress and return to their idols once relieved. Their love is fragmented and fickle. Those who believe direct all their love toward Allah and love created beings for the sake of Allah. They also love their leaders solely for the pleasure of Allah. Otherwise, they cannot escape wrongdoing and polytheism.\n\nWhen it is said in the Quran, 'Follow what Allah has revealed,' some say, 'We follow what we found our forefathers upon.' This is blind imitation (taqlid). Even if their forefathers lacked knowledge and were misguided, following them defies reason. What is right is to follow Allah's proof. Imitation without knowledge and proof is following Satan. The condition of such disbelievers is like that of animals that only hear a call and cry without understanding.\n\nO you who believe! Eat of the lawful and pure provisions given to you, and be grateful to Allah. Allah has forbidden you only carrion (dead meat), blood, the flesh of swine, and that which is slaughtered in the name of other than Allah. But in cases of necessity, without exceeding rights and without surpassing the measure of necessity, there is no sin in eating. Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.\n\nIn the verses, the importance of eating lawful and pure foods and expressing gratitude to Allah is emphasized first. It is stated that those who show ingratitude for blessings cannot be freed from physical and spiritual impurity. Quranic commentators explain that the command 'eat' signifies permissibility (mubah), whereas the command 'be grateful' signifies obligation (wajib). It is noted that the act of eating has degrees such as obligatory (fard), recommended (mandub), permissible (mubah), and forbidden (haram): eating enough to prevent death is obligatory, eating to gain strength for worship is recommended, eating until fully satisfied is permissible, and eating beyond that is forbidden.\n\nThe things forbidden by Allah are: carrion (dead meat), flowing blood, the flesh of swine, and animals slaughtered in the name of other than Allah. 'Ihlal' stems from the polytheists raising their voices in the name of their idols during slaughter; over time it acquired the general meaning of slaughter. In cases of necessity, there is a dispensation (rukhsah) to consume these forbidden items, provided that one does not infringe upon another's right and restricts the intake to the amount necessary to avert death. The legal maxims 'Necessities make forbidden things permissible' and 'Necessity is measured by its extent' are derived from these verses.\n\nVerses 174–176 condemn those who conceal the Book revealed by Allah to obtain worldly gain. They are as if filling their bellies with fire; on the Day of Resurrection, Allah will not speak to them nor purify them, and for them is a painful punishment. They have exchanged guidance for misguidance and forgiveness for punishment. Those who differ over the Book (such as those who believe in the Torah and the Gospel but reject the Quran) are in deep schism far from the truth. The verse emphasizes that true unity among people is possible only through belief in the true Book.\n\nIn verse 177, true righteousness (birr) is defined: It is not turning one's face toward the east or the west, but believing in Allah, the Last Day, the angels, the Book, and the prophets; giving wealth out of love for Him to relatives, orphans, the needy, the traveler, beggars, and for freeing captives; establishing prayer and paying zakah; fulfilling promises when made; and being patient in poverty, illness, and during warfare. It is these who are the truthful and it is these who are the righteous. The verse encompasses all the noble virtues of humanity.\n\nIn verses 178–179, legal retribution (qisas—life for life) is made obligatory: the free for the free, the slave for the slave, the woman for the woman. However, if the guardian of the slain forgives or accepts blood money (diyah), retribution is dropped; it is then necessary to act according to custom and pay the blood money with fairness. This is an alleviation and a mercy. Whoever transgresses after the pardon shall have a painful punishment. There is life in retribution, O people of understanding! The verse abolishes the inequalities of pre-Islamic ignorance (Jahiliyyah) and establishes equality in the security of life. Retribution is only applied in intentional killing; accidental killing is subject to a different ruling. There are differing views among legal schools (madhhabs) on whether a free person can be executed in retribution for a slave, or a man for a woman; the Hanafis apply retribution based on the principle of the inherent equality of human life.\n\nIn Surah al-Baqarah, verse 178, while the ruling of retribution (qisas) is explained, the matters of pardon and blood money (diyah) are also addressed. Accordingly, if the guardian (heir) of the slain person pardons the killer unconditionally (in an absolute manner) without stipulating any conditions, he must abide by this pardon and not demand blood money or anything else in return. If the pardon is granted in exchange for a specified amount of blood money or other property, it becomes obligatory (wajib) upon the killer to accept this compensation and pay it in a fair and proper manner. This regulation aims to establish justice between the parties and prevent blood feuds.\n\nThe expression 'from his brother' mentioned in the verse refers to the guardian, that is, the heir of the slain person. Describing the killer here as the brother of the victim's guardian is in the context of brotherhood in religion or a shared homeland. This characterization indicates that the lives and the rights to life of all individuals in Islamic society—whether free or slave, female or male, Muslim or dhimmi (non-Muslim subject)—are fraternally inviolable and sacrosanct. At the same time, it carries a profound human, moral, and social meaning that encourages the guardian to pardon.\n\nIf the guardian of the slain person decides to pardon, he should act with this consciousness of brotherhood, not go to excess in demanding blood money, and make his demand in accordance with recognized customary fairness (ma'ruf). The killer, recognizing the value of this pardon, must also pay his debt handsomely and without causing hardship. This ruling is a lightening and a mercy from your Lord. Whoever transgresses the limit after this ruling (for instance, by killing the murderer after having pardoned him), for him there is retribution in this world and a painful punishment in the Hereafter. The primary ruling is retribution; pardon and blood money, however, are a facilitation and a mercy.\n\nThe wisdom behind the legitimacy of retribution is emphasized by the verse, 'There is life for you in retribution, O people of understanding!' Retribution serves to preserve life both by deterring prospective killers and by ensuring the security of life in society. This aphorism is more eloquent and comprehensive than similar sayings in Arabic literature, because it clearly expresses that retribution is not aimed at killing, but on the contrary, at preserving life. Were it not for retribution, pardon would also have no meaning.\n\nIn verses 180–182, the subject of bequests (wasiyyah) is addressed. When someone is afflicted with a terminal illness, if they are to leave behind wealth (khayr), making a bequest to parents and close relatives in a recognized, proper manner (ma'ruf) is prescribed as a duty and an obligation upon those who fear Allah. Whoever alters this bequest after hearing it, the sin of it falls solely upon them. However, if someone fears a mistake or sin on the part of the testator and reconciles between the parties, there is no blame upon him. Allah is Most Forgiving, Most Merciful.\n\nScholars have differed as to whether the command of bequest in this verse was abrogated (naskh) by the verses of inheritance. According to the majority, a bequest to a relative who is an heir was abrogated, whereas a bequest to a relative who is not an heir, while not obligatory, is recommended (mustahabb). A group of scholars (such as Hasan al-Basri and Tawus) contend that a bequest to a relative who is not an heir is still obligatory. In conclusion, making a bequest to a non-inheriting relative is at least recommended (mandub) and is permissible within one-third of the estate.\n\nAbu Muslim al-Isfahani claimed that there is no abrogation (naskh) in the Quran. However, he was not consistent in this view and ultimately had to concede that the inheritance verses partially abrogated the bequest verse. Rather than calling this 'abrogation', he termed it 'specification' (takhsis). Yet specification is made between two proofs whose dates of revelation are unknown, whereas it is certain that the inheritance verses were revealed later. In this case, the operation carried out is not specification but partial abrogation, which is the opinion of a minority school of thought.\n\nGreat scholars such as al-Jassas have fiercely criticized those who deny abrogation in the Quran. According to them, both the predecessors (Salaf) and successors (Khalaf) of the Ummah accepted abrogation and entertained no doubt regarding it. Rejecting abrogation is akin to rejecting the Quran's general ('amm), specific (khass), clear (muhkam), and allegorical (mutashabih) verses. Such a claim is a form of fanaticism put forward by individuals devoid of sound knowledge, leading to a distortion of religious rulings.\n\nIn conclusion, Abu Muslim is the only person among Muslim scholars to deny abrogation. Even according to his view, it is not possible to act upon the bequest verse on its own. The ruling in this verse was entirely abrogated by the inheritance verses. However, other indications in the verse may be acted upon within the principles of jurisprudence (usul). For instance, there is no abrogation in verses 181–182 concerning the alteration of a bequest; these rulings remain valid.\n\nAllah warned those who alter a bequest, stating that doing so is a grave sin. However, if a bequest has deviated from the truth and entails injustice, rectifying it is permissible. In that case, if someone reconciles the parties and turns the bequest toward justice, he commits no sin, for Allah is Most Forgiving, Most Merciful. This permission is a concession (rukhsah), not a source of reward (thawab). Fulfilling a bequest, on the other hand, is a tremendous responsibility.\n\nNext, Allah declares that He has made the act of fasting obligatory. Fasting is an act of worship that was also prescribed for previous communities, serving to discipline the servants of Allah. It is proclaimed to the believers: 'Fasting is prescribed for you so that you may attain protection (and piety).' Fasting curbs the desires of the lower self (nafs), teaches patience, and leads to God-consciousness (taqwa). Through this act of worship, a person is freed from enslavement to worldly pleasures and draws closer to Allah.\n\nFasting has been made obligatory only for a designated number of days. Those who are ill or on a journey make up the missed days later. Those who can fast only with extreme difficulty pay a ransom (fidyah). Increasing the ransom is good, but fasting is more virtuous. The legal definition of fasting in the Sharia is to abstain with intention from food, drink, and sexual intercourse from the break of dawn (imsak) until sunset. The measure of travel that qualifies for the journey dispensation is a three-day distance of normal walking; modern conveyances are also evaluated according to their customary status.\n\nIn this section, the commentary on the verses of Surah al-Baqarah concerning fasting continues. First, the meaning of the word \"yutiquna\" is addressed. This word means not simply 'to be capable of,' but rather 'to endure with difficulty, to have one's strength depleted.' Therefore, the verse was revealed not concerning those who lack the capability to fast altogether, but regarding those who experience extreme difficulty in fasting or cannot endure it at all. These individuals are chronically ill persons with no hope of recovery and the elderly of advanced age.\n\nThe variant recitations (qira'at) of the verse are also addressed. In the recitations of Nafi', Abu Ja'far, and Ibn 'Amir, the word \"fidyah\" is used in the sense of 'feeding a poor person.' The amount of the fidyah is determined by legal standards: half a sa' of wheat, or one sa' of other foodstuffs. This measure also forms the basis for the charity of Fitr (sadaqat al-fitr) and expiations (kaffarat).\n\nCommentators have differed regarding whom the verse addresses. According to one view, following the concession regarding the sick and the traveler, the verse encompasses those who are similar to them but lack the possibility of making up missed days. According to another view, the verse granted a choice to those who were healthy and resident, but this ruling was later abrogated by the verse 'Whoever of you witnesses the month, let him fast it.' Imam Malik, on the other hand, interpreted the verse as referring to those who have an outstanding debt from a past Ramadan and lack the capacity to make it up.\n\nAccording to the preferred view, the verse is not abrogated. Many Companions, foremost among them Ibn Abbas, stated that the verse concerns very elderly men and women and that its ruling remains in effect. The Hanafi school is also of this opinion. Accordingly, those who can barely endure fasting in terms of both performance and making up missed fasts pay a ransom (fidyah). This ransom is legislated because fasting is an impossibility for them.\n\nThen, the passage moves to verse 185. In this verse, the month of Ramadan is described, and it is emphasized as the month in which the Quran was revealed. The literal and technical meanings of the word \"shahr\" (month) are explained. It is discussed whether the word Ramadan is one of the names of Allah or merely the name of a month. Furthermore, views regarding the derivation of the name Ramadan are addressed: it is reported that it is called by this name because it is a month that cleanses sins.\n\nThere are various opinions regarding the origin of the word Ramadan. According to the majority, it comes from \"ramad\" (ramad)—that is, the scorching of stones from the intense heat of the sun. The reason this month is named Ramadan is either the heat of hunger and thirst endured during fasting, or that fasting burns away sins. According to another view, the Arabs anciently named the months according to the seasons, and since this month coincided with intense heat, it was called Ramadan. Others say that Ramadan is derived from \"ramad\" in the sense of whetting and sharpening weapons, and that the Arabs prepared for battle in this month. It has also been narrated that the name Ramadan is among the beautiful names of Allah. In short, Ramadan carries meanings such as cleansing, burning, and sharpness.\n\nIn a hadith, it was stated regarding the month of Ramadan: \"Its beginning is mercy, its middle is forgiveness, and its end is salvation from the Fire.\" The most blessed night of this month is the Night of Decree (Laylat al-Qadr), and the Quran began to be revealed on this night. The word Quran is read in two ways: with a hamzah, derived from \"qira'ah\" (recitation), and without a hamzah, from the root \"qarn\" (conjoining). Imam al-Shafi'i stated that its form without a hamzah is not derived from another word, like the Torah and the Gospel, but is a proper name for the Book of Allah.\n\nIt is disputed whether the phrase \"a fixed number of days\" refers to Ramadan or to earlier fasts. According to Qatadah and 'Ata', this refers to the initial fast—that is, three days of every month or the Day of Ashura—after which Ramadan was made obligatory. However, according to the majority of scholars, the initial fast was established by the Sunnah, while the fast of Ramadan began with the verse \"Fasting is prescribed for you.\" The intent of \"a fixed number of days\" is also Ramadan. The revelation of the Quran in the month of Ramadan has been interpreted in three ways: it was sent down all at once to the worldly heaven and then revealed piecemeal; it began to be revealed in this month; or it was stated as praise for this month.\n\nThe word \"shuhud\" (witnessing) mentioned in the verse has three different meanings: first, being resident during the month of Ramadan; second, knowing the month with certainty; and third, sighting the crescent moon. Accordingly, whoever witnesses Ramadan must fast. The hadiths regarding the sighting of the crescent moon are as follows: \"Do not begin fasting until you see the crescent moon. If it is overcast, complete the month to thirty.\" Sighting the crescent moon at the beginning of Ramadan is essential. When the sky is clear, if only a few people see it while others do not, the testimony of these individuals is deemed refuted. In overcast weather, however, the testimony of a single person is sufficient. According to the Hanafis, one upright witness is required for Ramadan, and two upright witnesses are required for Eid.\n\nScholars hold differing views regarding the determination of the crescent moon through calculation. Astronomical calculation is not based on sighting the crescent moon; therefore, it has not been accepted as a legal (shar'i) criterion. Moreover, calculation is not known to everyone, which contradicts the Islamic principle of ease. For this reason, the beginning of fasting has remained tied to sighting the crescent moon. However, if in our day the obligation of searching for the crescent moon is neglected, it may be possible to benefit from astronomical calculation. Nevertheless, Muslims should not forget the duty of observing the crescent moon.\n\nFasting is obligatory upon every accountable (mukallaf) believer. Those who are ill or traveling may refrain from fasting during Ramadan and make up for it later. This is a concession (rukhsah), not a strict obligation ('azimah). For the elderly and the sick who have no hope of recovery, fidya is given. Allah desires ease, not hardship. These rulings were laid down to complete the number of days of the Ramadan fast and to give thanks to Allah. Through fasting, the soul is disciplined, patience is learned, and happiness in the Hereafter is attained.\n\nIn the prescription of the Ramadan fast, a fixed number of days was taken as the basis. Although the sighting of the month is an overarching cause, the detailed cause is each individual day. For this reason, fasting was first made obligatory as a fixed number of days, and the month served as its clarification. The expression \"so that you may complete the number\" linked the month to the number of days. Consequently, whoever breaks the fast for a few days during Ramadan is not required to make up the entire month, but only the days they did not fast. Likewise, for those whose status of accountability changes during Ramadan (such as those reaching puberty or those embarking on a journey), the ruling is shaped accordingly.\n\nFollowing the fast, the command to glorify Allah with takbir is emphasized. Takbir means magnifying Allah, and it occurs in three ways: through belief with the heart, affirmation with words, and worship through deeds. The takbir in this verse is an expression of completing the count of Ramadan and reaching Eid. For this takbir, recited with the words \"Allahu Akbar,\" it is commendable (mustahabb) to utter it silently when the crescent of Shawwal is sighted, and it is Sunnah to utter it aloud while going to the Eid prayer. According to the preferred view in the Hanafi school, none of these takbirs is obligatory (wajib).\n\nThe scope of the takbir in the verse also includes the Eid prayer and the takbirs recited therein. The expression \"so that you may give thanks\" indicates that the joy of Eid should be observed as an occasion of gratitude. Thus, the act of worship through fasting is brought in line with the command \"eat and give thanks.\" Furthermore, in these sentences there are proofs that refute the Jabriyyah sect.\n\nIn verse 186, when His servants ask about Him, Allah Almighty responds by saying, \"I am near; I answer the call of the caller when he calls upon Me.\" This nearness is not a spatial nearness, but rather signifies answering supplication swiftly. The verse refutes the naturalists, who regard Allah as a blind force, and those who deny the origin of divine decree. There is also a warning for those who assume Allah to be distant and shout in their supplications; humility is essential when supplicating.\n\nThe doubts raised regarding supplication are answered. That divine decree has already been determined does not render supplication unnecessary, for supplication is itself among the causes decreed in destiny. Allah's knowledge encompasses all things, yet supplication is a demonstration of servitude. A person both submits with contentment to the divine decree and makes supplication; these two states are not contradictory. Supplication is the essence of worship, and this verse, following the command of fasting, reinforces the importance of the fast.\n\nThe nearness of Allah is a profound subject debated by philosophers and theologians. In the words of al-Razi, Allah is nearer to the essence of every contingent being than that essence's own existence. This means that just as there can be no existence without Allah, there can also be no logic without Allah. The statement in the verse saying \"I am near\" rather than \"My servants are near to Me\" indicates that nearness originates from Allah, not from the servant. When the person who supplicates detaches from everything other than Allah, they have truly supplicated and attained Allah's nearness.\n\nThe occasion of revelation for the verse on fasting is as follows: The early Muslims could eat, drink, and be intimate with their spouses in the evening only until they performed the night prayer ('isha) or fell asleep. One night, after 'isha, Umar was intimate with his wife, felt remorse, and presented the matter to the Prophet. Thereupon, verse 187 of al-Baqarah was revealed. With this verse, it was made lawful to be intimate with spouses during the nights of fasting, and permission was given to eat and drink until the white thread of dawn becomes distinct from the black thread. In addition, the analogy was drawn that spouses are a garment for one another.\n\nThe word \"rafath\" (rafath) in this ayah signifies sexual intercourse. Allah, wishing ease for the Ummah, excluded the night from fasting and made sexual intercourse lawful. The command of \"mubasharah\" also indicates permissibility. However, the expression \"seek what Allah has decreed for you\" points out that the purpose of intimacy should be offspring. The white thread is the true dawn (al-fajr al-sadiq); the time of imsak begins with its rising. The false dawn (al-fajr al-kadhib), on the other hand, is the deceptive dawn and is not the criterion for beginning the fast.\n\nThe ayah is also evidence that the fast of one who enters the morning in a state of major ritual impurity (janabah) is valid. Because when sexual intercourse is permitted at night, postponing the ritual bath (ghusl) until morning also becomes permissible. The obligatory components (fard) of fasting are three: time, intention, and abstention (imsak). The time is from the break of dawn until the setting of the sun. Intention is required. Fasting is not only abstaining from eating, drinking, and sexual intercourse, but at the same time guarding organs such as the tongue, eyes, and ears from sin. Nevertheless, the obligatory imsak applies only to eating, drinking, and sexual intercourse. The others are recommended (mandub).\n\nWhile in a state of i'tikaf (seclusion) in mosques, sexual intercourse with spouses is forbidden. I'tikaf is staying in a mosque with the intention of worship. In the legal (shar'i) i'tikaf, fasting is a condition, and for this reason, it must last at least one day. Our Prophet (peace be upon him) regularly observed i'tikaf during the last ten days of Ramadan. I'tikaf is an emphasized Sunnah (sunnah al-mu'akkadah); it is not obligatory (wajib). However, continually abandoning it is not viewed favorably.\n\nIn ayah 188 of al-Baqarah, consuming wealth through wrongful means is prohibited. This encompasses situations such as bribery and unlawful gain. Wealth becomes unlawful (haram) either due to a quality inherent to it (for example, a poisonous or prohibited animal) or due to a flaw in the method of acquiring it. After undergoing self-discipline through fasting, believers should not unjustly reach for the property of others. Bribing judges leads to consuming property unjustly. Societies that abide by right and justice find peace.\n\nThere are narrations concerning the occasion of revelation of this ayah. In one of them, in a land dispute between Abdan al-Hadrami and Imru' al-Qays, when the Messenger of Allah proposed an oath, Imru' al-Qays heard the ayah and relinquished his claim. In another narration, two disputants came for judgment, and the Prophet said: \"I am only a human being; I judge according to your arguments. Whomever I judge in favor of regarding his brother's right, I have only given him a piece of fire,\" whereupon the parties wept and relinquished their claims.\n\nAyah 189 of al-Baqarah answers the question asked about the new moons (crescents): The new moons are measurements of time for the people and for Hajj. During the Age of Ignorance (Jahiliyyah), they considered it righteousness to enter houses from the back door while in ihram. Islam corrected this misconception, declaring that true righteousness is to possess piety (taqwa). Believers should enter their houses through their doors and be mindful of Allah. This ayah both points to the importance of the crescent in determining the times of worship and rejects superstitious customs.\n\nOne day, while in ihram, the Messenger of Allah entered through the gate of a ruined vineyard, and a man, seeing him, followed behind him. When the man was reminded that he was in ihram, he said, 'I am content with your Sunnah; you entered, so I entered.' This incident, together with a question regarding the crescents, became the occasion for the revelation of the ayah, and such awkward customs of the Jahiliyyah were abolished. In response, it was stated: The crescents are measurements of time for the people and for Hajj. Thanks to the phases of the moon, the number of years and the reckoning of time are known; this is also explained in Surahs Yunus and al-Isra.\n\nThere are many religious and worldly benefits in reckoning time by lunar months. The religious ones are: acts of worship such as the Ramadan fast, Hajj, the waiting period (iddah) of death, the iddah of menopause, and vows. The worldly ones are matters such as debts, rents, promises, and the gestation period. The changing of the moon in crescent phases is more convenient for reckoning time than the sun; through its relationship to night and day, the reckoning of the week has also been adapted to it. In addition, the transformation of the moon is proof that celestial bodies also undergo change and can perish. In this respect, the moon is an important sign illustrating Allah's power and unity.\n\nAfter this explanation, righteousness is not entering houses from their rears; rather, righteousness is that of one who fears Allah and protects himself. That is, the people of piety (taqwa) are those who believe and do good deeds. Asking the Prophet about the astronomical causes of the crescent is treating him like an astronomer and straining revelation unnecessarily. What truly ought to be done is to abandon Jahiliyyah customs and turn to Allah with piety. Enter houses through their doors, begin affairs from the proper way, and do not occupy yourselves with unnecessary questions.\n\nWith ayah 190 comes the command to fight: Fight in the way of Allah those who fight against you, but do not commit unjust aggression. Muslims were initially forbidden from fighting; then, with this ayah, permission was granted for defensive fighting. Although some narrations state that the first ayah on fighting is the verse of permission in Surah al-Hajj, the majority hold the view that this ayah is the first command to fight. This command is a previously promised permission and fulfilled the good tidings in previous scriptures that the final prophet would wage jihad with the sword.\n\nWhether this ayah was abrogated (mansukh) by subsequent ayahs is disputed. According to one view, the ayah is solely for defense and was abrogated by the command of total warfare in Surah at-Tawbah. According to the other view, the ayah is decisive (muhkam) because it encompasses both defense and offense, and subsequent ayahs are explanatory. Al-Raghib says: First, leniency was commanded, then permission to fight was given, and then came the command to fight against those who turn away from the truth. This is a requirement of gradual policy.\n\nEuropeans spread two contradictory ideas regarding Islam: the first is portraying it as aggressive by claiming that Islam spread by the sword; the second is aiming to disarm it by asserting that war in Islam is exclusively defensive. Both views are incorrect. The truth is that in Islam, both defense and, when necessary, offense in the way of Allah are legitimate. However, every war must be in the way of Allah, and unjust aggression and excess are forbidden. These ayahs regulated fighting in the Sacred Precinct (Haram) and during the sacred months, aiming to remove obstacles to visiting the Kaaba following the Treaty of Hudaybiyyah.\n\nAyah 191 says: kill them wherever you overtake them, and expel them from wherever they expelled you. Fitnah (persecution/discord) is more severe than killing. Fitnah deprives a person of religion and worldly life; therefore, fighting is necessary to eradicate fitnah. Do not fight them near al-Masjid al-Haram unless they fight you there; but if they fight you, retaliate. These rulings warn believers against fitnah and encourage becoming a ghazi or a martyr in the way of Allah.\n\nIt is not permissible to kill by way of aggression within the sanctuary (haram) of the Kaaba and Mecca the Blessed (Makkah al-Mukarramah). The primary duty is solely to expel them. However, whoever kills there is killed. In fact, if someone commits a murder within Mecca and takes refuge in the sanctuary of the Kaaba, it is still not permissible to kill him there; he is taken out and legal retribution (qisas) is carried out. The verses, \"And remember when We made the House a place of return for people and a sanctuary secure [from any enemy aggression]\" (Al-Baqarah, 2/125) and \"Whoever enters it is safe [from any violation]\" (Al 'Imran, 3/97) support this ruling. However, whoever commits murder within the Sacred Sanctuary (al-Haram al-Sharif) is killed there; this command specifies (takhsis) the preceding part. It is stated in \"Ahkam al-Qur'an\" that: since there can be no abrogating (nasikh) and abrogated (mansukh) elements within a single address, and both the unity of recitation and the order of revelation indicate that these two verses were revealed in a single address, and with no authentic transmission to the contrary existing, just as the expression 'Kill them wherever you find them' does not abrogate the ruling of what precedes it but rather explains it, likewise this prohibition does not abrogate the entirety of the command 'Kill them,' but specifies it. Furthermore, this prohibition has not been abrogated by any command; it is definitive (muhkam).\n\nWhen the expression in verse 192, \"If they cease [their disbelief], then indeed, Allah is Forgiving and Merciful,\" is evaluated together with the stipulation \"Expel them from wherever they have expelled you,\" it is understood that this verse does not concern the People of the Book, but is specific to the Arab polytheists. While submission with the poll-tax (jizya) is acceptable from the People of the Book, the Arab polytheists are bound either to accept Islam or face the sword. In verse 193, it is stated: \"Fight them until there is no fitnah (polytheism and discord) and religion is entirely for Allah.\" The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: \"I have been commanded to fight these people until they say 'La ilaha illallah.' When they say it, they protect their lives from me.\" While others could safeguard their blood with jizya, this permission was not granted to the Arab polytheists. If they desist from disbelief and accept Islam, then there is no hostility except against the oppressors.\n\nIn verse 194, the issue of the sacred month is addressed: \"The sacred month is for the sacred month, and for violations there is legal retribution (qisas).\" This means: whoever attacks you and violates something of your sanctity and inviolability, retaliate against them to the same extent they attacked you. \"The retribution for an evil deed is an evil one like it\" (Ash-Shura, 42/40). The one who initiates evil is more unjust; retaliation is not transgression, but the punishment for transgression. Retribution (qisas) expresses responding in kind. In accordance with this general rule, the sacred month is likewise exchanged for the same sacred month. In the year of Hudaybiyyah, in the month of Dhu al-Qa'dah, the enemies violated the sanctity of the sacred month by hurling stones and arrows at the Muslims; you, too, by retaliating in kind, do not refrain from fighting in the same month of Dhu al-Qa'dah. As the occasion of revelation (sabab al-nuzul) of the verse, it is mentioned that the polytheists plotted to alter the treaty during the sacred month, or that the Quraysh turned the Messenger of Allah back from Mecca in the month of Dhu al-Qa'dah. In any case, these verses were revealed in the year of the fulfilled 'umrah (the 7th year of the Hijrah). Although the \"custom of the sacred months\" was later abrogated unconditionally, the general rulings regarding retaliating in kind remain in force.\n\nIn verse 195, spending in the way of Allah (infaq) is commanded: \"And spend in the way of Allah and do not throw yourselves into destruction with your own hands.\" The occasion of revelation of this verse was to serve as a reminder that abandoning jihad is a peril. According to a narration, during a campaign against Constantinople in the Umayyad era, when a Muslim charged at the enemy in the fortress, the crowd said, \"He is throwing himself into destruction.\" Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, however, stated that this verse was revealed concerning the Ansar who said, \"Let us stay with our properties and occupy ourselves with putting them in order,\" and he pointed out that throwing oneself into destruction was abandoning jihad. There are also different interpretations of the verse, such as wasting one's wealth through extravagance and perishing, or rushing into battle in a way that has no impact on the enemy. As Imam Muhammad stated in his \"Siyar al-Kabir,\" a single person charging against a thousand people is permissible if there is hope of survival or breaking the enemy, but otherwise it is reprehensible (makruh). In any case, causing one's own death without any benefit to the religion or the believers is not appropriate; yet if there is a benefit for the faith, it is an honorable station.\n\nVerse 196 brings the rulings concerning Hajj and 'umrah: \"And complete the Hajj and 'umrah for Allah. But if you are prevented, then [send] what can be obtained with ease of sacrificial animals...\" This verse was also revealed during the year of the fulfilled 'umrah. The definitions, pillars (arkan), conditions, obligations (wajibat), sunnahs, and prohibitions of Hajj and 'umrah are explained in detail in books of jurisprudence (fiqh). The obligation of Hajj primarily indicates through this verse that completing a Hajj and 'umrah once begun is obligatory; its fundamental obligation, however, was established later with Al 'Imran 97. The wisdom of Hajj encompasses numerous religious and worldly benefits; it is the greatest act of worship that practically manifests social unity. Hajj and 'umrah also carry the meaning of jihad. The verse also legislated the tamattu' and qiran forms of Hajj and made the sacrifice obligatory. There are three types of Hajj: ifrad, tamattu', and qiran.\n\nThe completion of Hajj and 'umrah is commanded. Since the completion of supererogatory (nafl) acts of worship once begun is obligatory, this command does not yet constitute definitive evidence that Hajj and 'umrah are fundamentally obligatory. The scholars differed on whether 'umrah is obligatory (wajib): Aisha, Ibn Abbas, Ibn Umar, Hasan al-Basri, and Imam al-Shafi'i held that 'umrah is obligatory; Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, Ibrahim al-Nakha'i, and al-Sha'bi stated that it is supererogatory. In the Hanafi school, 'umrah is not separately obligatory (fard); if begun as a voluntary act, completing it becomes obligatory (wajib).\n\nIhsar is being prevented from fulfilling the pillars of Hajj or 'umrah after entering ihram. Obstacles such as an enemy, illness, or disability are considered ihsar. A person subjected to ihsar sends a sacrificial animal to the Sanctuary (Haram) in order to exit ihram, and once the presumption that the sacrifice has been slaughtered is formed, they may shave their head and exit ihram. According to the Hanafis, the sacrifice must be slaughtered within the Haram; al-Shafi'i and Malik stated that the sacrifice may be slaughtered at the place of prevention (ihsar), yet authentic narrations indicate that the Prophet (peace be upon him) slaughtered his sacrifice in the Haram.\n\nIf it becomes necessary to shave the head while in ihram due to an ailment such as lice or wounds on the head, one chooses as a ransom (fidyah) between fasting for three days, feeding six poor persons, or sacrificing a sheep. This ruling applies not only to ihsar, but to any situation of illness and affliction. In addition, other prohibitions of ihram, such as wearing stitched garments or using perfume, similarly fall under concession (rukhsah) through a ransom.\n\nThose who perform the tamattu' and qiran forms of Hajj—that is, those who combine 'umrah and Hajj—are obligated to slaughter a sacrificial animal that can be obtained with ease. According to al-Shafi'i, this sacrifice is to compensate for a deficiency, and its owner may not eat from it; according to the Hanafis, it is a sacrifice of thanksgiving, and its owner may eat from it. Those who cannot find a sacrificial animal fast for ten days: three days during Hajj and seven days after returning. This ruling applies to those who do not reside around the Sacred Mosque (al-Masjid al-Haram)—that is, the pilgrims coming from beyond the miqat (afaqis).\n\nHajj is during well-known months: Shawwal, Dhu al-Qa'dah, and ten days of Dhu al-Hijjah. Whoever intends to perform Hajj during these months must refrain from sexual relations, committing sins, and disputing during Hajj. Taking provisions has been commanded, and it has been declared that the best provision is God-consciousness (taqwa). Taqwa provides protection from evil and is the most important preparation for both journeys (this worldly life and the Hereafter).\n\nRemember Allah at Muzdalifah after descending from Arafat. During the Age of Ignorance (Jahiliyyah), the Quraysh, considering themselves superior, would not go up to Arafat and would make the standing (waqfah) only at Muzdalifah. With this verse, everyone was commanded to come from Arafat, emphasizing equality. Whoever does not perform the standing at Arafat on the Day of Arafah has not attained the Hajj.\n\nWhen you have completed your rites of Hajj, remember Allah as you remember your fathers, or with even greater remembrance. Among people are those who pray only for this world, and they have no share in the Hereafter; while others say, \"Our Lord, give us good in this world and good in the Hereafter, and protect us from the punishment of the Fire.\" For these, there is a share of what they have earned. Allah is swift in reckoning.\n\nAccording to Imam Fakhr al-Din al-Razi, happiness has three levels: pertaining to the soul, pertaining to the body, and external. Happiness pertaining to the soul is related to knowledge and good character; that pertaining to the body is related to health and beauty; and external happiness is related to wealth and status. Those who say, \"Give to us in this world\" may ask for any of these aspects. However, if knowledge and morality are sought for worldly gain, they become worldly; and those who do not believe in the Hereafter ask only for this world.\n\nThe prayer of the believers is as follows: \"Our Lord! Give us good in this world and good in the Hereafter, and protect us from the punishment of the Fire.\" Hasana (good/blessing) is every blessing that brings joy to a person's soul, body, or circumstances. There is a distinction between hasan, hasana, and husna: while hasan is used for both concrete entities and abstract meanings, hasana is used for spiritual matters, and husna is used exclusively in spiritual contexts. People of intellect and insight, considering both the beginning and the end, ask for goodness in both this world and the Hereafter.\n\nPeople are of three kinds: some desire only this world, some desire both this world and the Hereafter, and some sacrifice their lives for the pleasure of Allah. Allah grants a share to each according to their desire; He is swift in reckoning. The phrase \"those\" encompasses both groups; indeed, as stated in Surah ash-Shura, whoever desires the harvest of the Hereafter, their harvest is increased, and whoever desires the harvest of this world, they are given of it, but they have no share in the Hereafter.\n\nThe numbered days are the days of tashriq, which are the eleventh, twelfth, and thirteenth days of Dhu al-Hijjah. Remembering Allah with takbir during these days has been commanded. The Day of Arafah and the day of Eid are also days of remembrance. Stoning the devil and reciting takbir after Hajj are among the significant acts of worship of these days. Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) would recite takbir in his tent and on the road, and the people would follow him. The command \"Remember Allah during the numbered days\" refers to the takbirs of tashriq.\n\nAmong people are those who utter pleasing words regarding the life of this world and call Allah to witness what is in their hearts, yet in reality they are the fiercest of adversaries. They cause corruption in the land and destroy crops and cattle. When it is said to them, \"Fear Allah,\" pride drives them to further sin. Hell is sufficient for them; what an evil resting place! These verses were revealed concerning al-Akhnas ibn Shariq, yet they encompass all hypocrites.\n\nIn contrast, among people are those who give their lives seeking the pleasure of Allah. These are Allah's distinguished servants; they endure hardships for the sake of religion and worship, and they strive (perform jihad). They pass from the pleased soul (nafs al-radiyyah) to the pleasing soul (nafs al-mardiyyah). Allah is compassionate to His servants and enjoins piety upon them. However, there are also those who, out of satanic arrogance, mistake their own whims for the pleasure of Allah; for this reason, the condition of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah should be compared with that of the Kharijites.\n\nO you who believe! Enter into peace and security in complete submission, and do not follow the footsteps of Satan. For Satan is an open enemy to you. Islam means submitting to Allah and entering into peace. A Muslim is one from whose tongue and hand others are safe. If you deviate from peace after clear proofs have come to you, know that Allah is Almighty, All-Wise.\n\nYou will not enter Paradise without being tested by hardships as were the nations before you. Such poverty and afflictions befell them that the messenger and those with him said, \"When is the help of Allah?\" Behold, the help of Allah is near.\n\nIn his commentary on verse 213 of Surah al-Baqarah, Elmalılı states that the expression 'Mankind was one community' in the verse indicates that humanity was initially united, that they subsequently fell into disagreement, and that Allah sent prophets. This meaning is supported by the context preceding and following the verse, as well as by Surah Yunus, verse 19, and the recitation (qira'ah) of Abdullah ibn Mas'ud. The first human beings lived as a single community starting from Adam, and differences arose later.\n\nAccording to the majority of tafsir scholars, this 'single community' was upon truth and monotheism (tawhid). By their very creation, they were inclined toward unity; polytheism (shirk) and disbelief (kufr) emerged only later. Proponents of this view narrated that this unity lasted for ten centuries from Adam to Noah, or that it consisted solely of Adam, Eve, and their offspring. The fundamental point is that tawhid is an eternal principle embedded in human innate nature (fitrah).\n\nSome scholars, such as Ikrimah and Qatadah, asserted that this single community was upon disbelief and falsehood. However, this view faces three objections: it would imply that the prophets were sent to create division, it contradicts the fact that Adam was both a prophet and a father, and it does not accord with the social meaning of the word 'ummah' (community). These objections render the majority opinion more coherent.\n\nAccording to a third view attributed to al-Maturidi, the earliest humans lived in a natural state, devoid of any divine law (shari'ah) and legal responsibility. When disputes arose with population growth, prophets were sent. This view has been criticized because it deprives 'ummah' of its social meaning and contradicts the reality that humans are inherently social by nature. The prohibition given to Adam, 'do not approach this tree,' shows that responsibility existed from the very beginning.\n\nQadi Abd al-Jabbar of the Mu'tazilah stated that prior to the prophets, people were a community governed by rational laws. Another group of scholars refrained from passing judgment on this matter, deferring the knowledge to Allah, while some limited the 'single community' to the peoples of Abraham and Moses (Musa). After pointing out the weaknesses of these views, Elmalılı emphasizes that the first view is more sound and concludes that the verse directs humanity toward monotheism and peace through the prophethood of Muhammad.\n\nAllah proclaims: O community of Muhammad! Did you think that you would enter Paradise merely by believing, without experiencing the afflictions and hardships that befell the communities before you? Those communities were shaken to such an extent that their prophet and the believers with him were brought to say, 'When is the help of Allah?' Know well that the help of Allah is certainly near. As long as you do not deviate from faith and guidance, you will attain that help. This verse was revealed in Medina due to the hostility of the Jews or because of the Battle of Uhud or the Battle of the Trench. By the course of history, this community will also encounter the hardships endured by previous nations, but those who are patient will succeed. The era of the Prophet will be the foundation for all standards; transgression will subsequently reappear, but through patience and effort, the partisans of truth will attain divine help, witness truth prevail, and establish universal peace.\n\nIn verse 215: \"O Muhammad! They ask you where they should spend. Say: Whatever good you spend is for parents, close relatives, orphans, the needy, and travelers. And whatever good you do, indeed Allah knows it.\" This verse was revealed upon the question of Amr ibn al-Jamuh, who was martyred in the Battle of Uhud. The priority of spending is first on parents, then close relatives, then orphans, the poor, and travelers. It becomes obligatory according to the degree of kinship. Allah also knows any other good deeds and will reward them.\n\nIn verse 216: \"Fighting is ordained for you, though you dislike it. It may be that you dislike a thing and it is good for you; and it may be that you love a thing and it is bad for you. Allah knows, and you know not.\" Fighting is an obligation that establishes general peace and unifies the truth. Sometimes it is an individual obligation (fard al-ayn), and sometimes a collective obligation (fard al-kifayah). Liking and disliking is an emotion; goodness and badness depend on outcomes. Allah informs [us of this] through His Book and historical examples. Since human history is filled with transgression and division, war is an inevitable necessity. Because Allah knows that the good of those committed to the truth lies in their protection, He made fighting an obligation. Martyrdom, victory, and spoils of war will be good.\n\nIn verse 217: \"O Muhammad! They ask you about fighting in the sacred month. Say: Fighting in it is a great sin; but keeping [people] from the path of Allah, disbelief in Him, preventing access to al-Masjid al-Haram, and expelling its people from it are greater sins. Persecution (fitnah) is worse than killing. They will fight you if they can to turn you away from your religion. Whoever of you turns back from his religion and dies as a disbeliever, their deeds will become worthless; they are the companions of the Fire, remaining there eternally.\" This verse was revealed regarding the raid that Abdullah ibn Jahsh carried out against a Quraysh caravan at Nakhlah. It was the month of Rajab at that time, and fighting had taken place during the sacred month. The Muslims hesitated, but the verse made it known that persecution is greater.\n\nIn verse 218: \"Indeed, those who have believed, those who have emigrated, and those who have striven in the cause of Allah hope for Allah's mercy. Allah is Most Forgiving, Most Merciful.\" In verse 219: \"O Muhammad! They ask you about wine and gambling. Say: In them is a great sin and some benefits for people, but their sin is greater than their benefit. They also ask you what they should spend; say: The surplus (that which exceeds necessity). Thus Allah clarifies the verses to you so that you may reflect.\" This verse was revealed upon the question of Umar and Mu'adh. The prohibition of wine came gradually; first it was declared a great sin through this verse, then approaching prayer while intoxicated was forbidden with an-Nisa 4:43, and finally it was rendered strictly forbidden with al-Ma'idah 5:90–91. Although the word 'khamr' is a specific name for grape wine, it generally encompasses everything that causes intoxication. According to Imam Malik and al-Shafi'i, every kind of intoxicant is forbidden and not even a single drop is permissible. While Imam Abu Hanifah stated that grape wine is forbidden in its essence, and other intoxicants are forbidden due to their intoxicating property—such that drinking a small quantity might be permissible—the preferred view is that whatever intoxicates in large amounts, a small amount of it is also forbidden. Gambling is likewise forbidden; lottery-type games such as the divining arrows (azlam) of the Jahiliyyah period are also gambling. In summary, this verse declares that wine and gambling are great sins; their benefits are temporary, while their harms are lasting.\n\nAcquire wealth through lawful (halal) earnings, not through illegitimate ways such as gambling. After providing for the needs of yourself and the family members you are obligated to support, spend the surplus in charitable causes. Allah makes His verses that explain His legal rulings known to you so that you may reflect and act accordingly.\n\nThese verses contain rulings for both this world and the Hereafter. The religion of Islam encompasses both realms. In this context, they ask you about the situation of orphans. According to narration, when the verse concerning orphans in Surah an-Nisa was revealed, people became hesitant to associate with orphans. Thereupon, this verse was revealed: Improving the affairs of orphans is better than neglecting them. If you live together with them, they are your brothers in faith. Allah knows the corruptor from the rectifier. Had He willed, He could have put you in hardship; approach orphans with the intention of betterment through the strength He has provided.\n\nDo not marry polytheist women until they believe. A believing bondwoman is better than a polytheist woman, even if she pleases you. And do not marry believing women to polytheist men; a believing slave is better than a polytheist man. Polytheists call you to the Fire, while Allah invites to Paradise and forgiveness. \"Polytheist\" (mushrik) here encompasses both open idolaters and those of the People of the Book who distort monotheism (tawhid). However, with the verse in Surah al-Ma'idah, permission was granted to marry women from the People of the Book; but giving believing women in marriage to men from the People of the Book is strictly forbidden.\n\nThey ask you about women's menstruation. Say: It is a harm. Stay away from women while they are in this state, and do not have sexual relations until they are cleansed. When they have cleansed themselves, approach them from where Allah has commanded you. Indeed, Allah loves those who repent and those who purify themselves. Women are a tilth for you; so go to your tilth however you wish, but prepare ahead for yourselves. Fear Allah and know that you will meet Him.\n\nDo not make Allah an obstacle in your oaths against doing good, acting righteously, and making peace between people. Allah does not hold you accountable for unintentional empty oaths, but He holds you accountable for deliberate false oaths made by your hearts. Those who swear not to approach their wives shall wait four months; if they return, Allah is Forgiving, and if they decide upon divorce, Allah is All-Hearing, All-Knowing. Divorced women shall wait for three menstrual cycles; their husbands have more right to take them back during this period if they desire reconciliation. Divorce is twice; then either retaining in honor or releasing in good will. After the third divorce, a woman is not lawful to her former husband until she marries another husband. These are the limits of Allah, so do not transgress them.\n\nAn inadvertent oath (laghw) is one lacking intent and resolve, where a person swears based on their conviction and then realizes they were mistaken. Imam al-Shafi'i also includes among inadvertent oaths words uttered for emphasis without any intention of swearing, such as \"No, by Allah\" and \"Yes, by Allah.\" The intent of not holding accountable in this verse refers to accountability in the Hereafter, not expiation (kaffarah) in this world. Allah does not hold one accountable for oaths made without knowing and assumed to be true; however, He holds one accountable for oaths sworn falsely and deliberately with the earning of the heart, which is called a false oath that plunges one into sin (yamin al-ghamus) and has no expiation.\n\nFor men who practice ila, that is, swear not to have sexual intercourse with their wives, there is a waiting period of four months. If they return during this period by breaking their oath (fay'), they are forgiven through expiation. If they do not return, they are considered to have resolved upon divorce. The early scholars (Salaf) differed on this matter: Ibn Abbas and a group stated that an irrevocable divorce (ba'in talaq) takes place at the end of four months, while a narration from Ali and Ibn Umar indicates that once the period expires, the husband is detained and compelled either to return or to divorce. The third view states that a revocable divorce (raj'i talaq) occurs at the end of four months. The Hanafis adopted the first view, while the Shafi'is and Malikis adopted the second view.\n\nDivorced women shall wait for three menstrual cycles or periods of purity (quru'). The word 'quru'' means both menstruation and the period of purity. The Hanafis, relying on hadiths transmitted from Aisha and Ibn Umar, interpreted this as menstruation. Women may not conceal the menstruation or pregnancy that Allah has created in their wombs. Their husbands have a greater right to take them back during the waiting period ('iddah) if they have divorced them with a revocable divorce (raj'i talaq). Men have a degree over women; this is because they bear the burdens of the family and provide maintenance.\n\nDivorce (talaq) is twice. After the second divorce, one must either retain the wife with kindness or release her with grace. It is not lawful to take back the dower (mahr) or anything else. However, if it is feared that the husband and wife will not be able to observe the bounds of Allah, it is permissible for the woman to seek divorce in exchange for compensation (khul'). This becomes an irrevocable divorce (ba'in talaq). According to a narration, Jamila sought khul' because she did not love her husband Thabit ibn Qays. These rulings are the bounds of Allah; whoever transgresses them, they are the wrongdoers.\n\nAfter the third divorce, the woman is not lawful for her first husband until she marries another husband. This prohibition continues until the woman genuinely marries another man and consummates the marriage with him. Allah permitted up to three divorces and arranged this as a trial period. When the waiting periods of divorced women come to an end, it is commanded either to retain them with kindness or release them. It is forbidden to take the verses of Allah in mockery. Furthermore, guardians may not prevent divorced women from remarrying their former husbands; this is better and purer.\n\nMothers suckle their children for two full years. The father is obligated to provide food and clothing for the nursing mother. No mother or father should be subjected to harm on account of their child. The parents may decide to wean the child through mutual consultation. It is also permissible to entrust the child to another wet nurse. In all these rulings, one must fear Allah and know that He sees all things.\n\nAllah Almighty commanded that mothers suckle their children for two full years. This period is for those who wish to complete the nursing. The father of the child is obligated to provide for the mother's food and clothing within his means. No one is burdened beyond their capacity. Neither mother nor father should be made to suffer harm on account of the child. If the father has passed away, this maintenance obligation falls upon the heir.\n\nIf the mother and father decide on weaning before two years through mutual consultation and with regard for the child's well-being, there is no sin upon them in this regard. However, if it is done without the consent of one of the parties, it becomes a sin. If the father wishes to hire a wet nurse for his child, he has the right to do so, provided he pays the fee according to customary practice. Allah is aware of all that you do.\n\nWomen whose husbands pass away observe a waiting period ('iddah) of four months and ten days. During this period, they may not marry, adorn themselves, or present themselves to suitors. When the waiting period ends, there is no sin upon them, as they may present themselves to suitors to marry in a lawful manner. The duration of the waiting period for death, unlike the waiting period for divorce, is equal for every woman, and this is based on a divine wisdom.\n\nYou may hint indirectly at your desire to marry women observing the waiting period for bereavement. However, it is not permissible to make secret agreements or promise intercourse. Do not resolve on the marriage contract until the waiting period has reached its term. Allah knows what is in your hearts, so beware of what is forbidden. Allah is Most Forgiving, Most Forbearing.\n\nThere is no sin upon you if you divorce a woman whose dower (mahr) has not been specified and with whom marriage has not yet been consummated. However, you must provide for her a compensatory gift (mut'ah) according to your means. If you have specified a mahr and divorce her before consummation, it is your duty to pay half of the mahr. Either the woman or the husband may waive this right; for the man to give more is closer to righteousness (taqwa). Do not forget graciousness between you.\n\nMaintain the prayers, especially the middle prayer (the afternoon prayer, 'Asr), and stand before Allah in devotion and humility. Even in a state of fear, perform the prayer on foot or while mounted. When you are safe, perform the prayer as usual. There are differing opinions as to which prayer is the middle prayer; the strongest narration indicates that it is the 'Asr prayer.\n\nThere are various opinions regarding the middle prayer. While some stated that it is the 'Asr (afternoon) prayer, some indicated that it is the Friday prayer, and others that it is the Fajr (dawn) prayer. The Prophet's (peace be upon him) hadith, 'They diverted us from the middle prayer,' points to the 'Asr prayer. However, which prayer is the middle prayer has not acquired certainty; this uncertainty is intended to ensure that care is given to all prayers.\n\nAccording to one view, the middle prayer was designated as the 'Asr prayer, but later this designation was lifted. Therefore, the soundest view is that any of the five daily prayers could be the middle prayer. Whichever prayer is the most challenging for a person is the middle prayer for them.\n\nAllah commanded: Guard the prayers, especially the middle prayer. In times of fear, the prayer may be performed on foot or while mounted, depending on the circumstances. In states of extreme fear, it is permissible to pray by gesturing (ima). When security is attained, prayers must be performed in full.\n\nMen who pass away are obligated to leave provision for their wives for one year. However, this ruling was subsequently abrogated by the inheritance verses. Divorced women are also granted a provision (mut'ah) according to customary practice. This is a duty upon those who fear Allah.\n\nMany people abandoned their homes out of fear of death, yet Allah said to them, 'Die,' and then brought them back to life. This shows that there is no escape from Allah's decree and that He has power to do whatever He wills. The believers must fight in the way of Allah and lend Him a goodly loan.\n\nAfter Moses (Musa), the Children of Israel asked one of their prophets for a commander to wage war. Saul (Talut) was chosen as their commander. There were those who looked down on him due to his lack of wealth and lineage. The sign of Saul was the Ark (tabut) that the angels would bring. The small group that passed the test of the river gained victory over Goliath (Jalut) by Allah's permission. David (Dawud) killed Goliath, and Allah granted him kingship and prophethood.\n\nThe Children of Israel objected to their prophet regarding Saul's becoming king, saying: 'We are more worthy of kingship than he is, and he has not been given wealth.' The prophet informed them that Allah had chosen him and granted him an abundance of knowledge and physical stature. What matters in kingship is not lineage, but merit and Allah's choice. Allah gives His kingdom to whom He wills; He knows all things and is all-encompassing in mercy.\n\nThe sign of Saul's kingship was the arrival of the lost Ark (Tabut). This Ark was a chest containing sacred relics left by the family of Moses and Aaron (Harun), and a tranquility and reassurance (sakinah). The sakinah was a spiritual support and a sign of victory for the Children of Israel. The arrival of the Ark was clear proof of the legitimacy of Saul's kingship.\n\nWhen Saul set out with his army, he tested his soldiers with a river, saying: 'Whoever drinks from this water is not of me, except the one who scoops up a handful.' Most of the soldiers disobeyed the order and drank from the river, and only a few remained with Saul. The thirst of those who drank only increased, and they had no strength left to fight. Thereupon, Saul and the believers with him prayed to Allah for patience and victory against Goliath and his army.\n\nAs a result of supplication, with Allah's permission, the enemy was routed and David killed Goliath. Allah granted David kingdom, wisdom, and prophethood. This story demonstrates that a small group, through faith and patience, can achieve great victories. Furthermore, it recounts that Allah maintains the order of the earth by repelling some people by means of others. The legitimacy of war is to prevent corruption and establish justice.\n\nNext, the degrees of the prophets are described: Allah favored some prophets over others. He spoke to Moses, gave clear miracles to Jesus, and supported him with the Holy Spirit (Ruh al-Qudus). The highest station, although not explicitly stated, belongs to the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him). When discussing the superiority of prophets, one must avoid excess and negligence (ifrat and tafrit).\n\nIn Ayat al-Kursi, Allah's oneness, life, self-subsistence (Qayyumiyyah), and the all-encompassing nature of His knowledge are emphasized. Then it is stated, 'There is no compulsion in religion'; because right guidance has become distinct from error. Whoever rejects taghut and believes in Allah has grasped the most trustworthy handhold that never breaks.\n\nThe superiorities and degrees of the prophets are divine gifts from Allah, not inherent attributes existing in their own persons. Prophethood is a deputation to convey and fulfill the divine commands. They are subsumed in the divine command; they cannot unconditionally do whatever they wish. Absolute will belongs to Allah.\n\nHad Allah willed, the communities after the prophets would not have shed each other's blood. In particular, Jesus' counsel, 'Turn the other cheek to the one who slaps your cheek,' forbids even retaliating against aggression. Yet despite this, discord and wars took place. This means that a divine command does not eliminate the possibility of acting contrary to it; a legislative command (amr taklifi) is not a creative, compelling will (iradah takwiniyyah).\n\nAllah did not will for discord to be absolutely nonexistent. The degrees of the prophets and the diversity of nations are by His will. Just as the command of Tawhid did not completely prevent discord, neither did it eliminate the possibility of opposition. It is precisely for this reason that jihad was made obligatory as a practical, active proof of the truth; it is meant to prevent disbelief and aggression.\n\nIn verse 254, those who believe in Allah and His messengers are commanded to spend from their wealth in the way of Allah. On the Day of Resurrection, neither bargaining, nor friendship, nor intercession will be of benefit. One must make preparation by giving zakah and charity before that day arrives. Those who disbelieve are wrongdoers; they do not heed abstract proofs, stopping only when confronted with a practical obstacle.\n\nAyat al-Kursi is a magnificent verse that introduces Allah in the most beautiful manner. There is no deity except Allah; He is the Ever-Living (al-Hayy) and the Self-Sustaining (al-Qayyum). Neither slumber nor heedlessness overtakes Him. To Him belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth. Who is it that can intercede with Him except by His permission? He knows what is before them and what is behind them. His Kursi encompasses the heavens and the earth, and guarding them does not tire Him. He is the Most High, the Most Great.\n\nIn the lexicon, kursi means that which is sat upon, a throne, a seat of knowledge, and similar meanings. Allah's Kursi expresses His dominion and the reign of His knowledge. Commentators have stated that the Kursi is a vast body beneath the Throne (Arsh) that encompasses the heavens and the earth. However, it is not possible to determine its true nature. What is essential is that Allah encompasses all things, and nothing can encompass Him.\n\nThe hadiths concerning the Kursi and the Throne (Arsh) explain by way of analogy that the Kursi is not the eighth heaven or the sphere of the fixed stars, but rather a ring that is vast in comparison to the heavens and the earth, yet small in comparison to the Throne. Imam Fakhr al-Din al-Razi, in his commentary on al-Fatihah, addresses the Kursi through theories of space and the Throne through theories of time. While absolute space encompasses the heavens and the earth, all spatial extensions fit into a single moment of time, and this circle of circumstance remains like a small ring in a vast desert. There are also narrations regarding the spiritual values of the Kursi and the Throne; while their essence is included within the boundary of the Throne, this stems not from the Kursi being corporeal, but from it being a stage for the manifestation of spiritual forces.\n\nThere are different views regarding the Kursi: First, Kursi means dominion, power, and sovereignty; for divinity is manifested only through power and creation. Second, Allah's Kursi means His knowledge; this view is narrated from Ibn Abbas and preferred by many commentators such as Ibn Jarir al-Tabari. Third, the purpose is solely to convey Allah's majesty and greatness; just as He designated the Kaaba as His house and made the Black Stone (al-Hajar al-Aswad) a place to be kissed, He addressed humanity in ways with which they are familiar. Just as corporeality is not intended in these analogies, the purpose in the Throne and the Kursi is likewise to convey Allah's majesty and greatness.\n\nAlthough it is certain that a Kursi in its known physical sense is not meant, it is obligatory to believe that Allah has a Kursi. That it involves a concept more or less related to physical form does not necessitate attributing corporeality to Allah. The attribution of the Kursi to Allah is not an 'attribution of sitting,' but an 'attribution of Lordship' (rububiyyah). As understood from the verse, Allah's Kursi is something that covers all of the heavens and the earth and encompasses all physical bodies. We believe in its existence and understand that we cannot know its true reality. We conceive of the Kursi as a locus for the manifestation of absolute knowledge and dominion, and we recognize with certainty that all bodies remain within it and that the One Who exercises authority over it is a Being far exalted above being a physical body.\n\nWhat is settled here is not Allah, but the totality of bodies termed the heavens and the earth. Above this is the Throne (Arsh), which encompasses the extension of time, the realm of intellects and souls, and is surrounded by the angels brought near (al-muqarrabun); this realm is beyond space and carries no meaning of corporeality. Allah Almighty is not within the Throne, but is above it with the attribute of Rahmaniyyah (All-Mercifulness); this being above is a transcendence that has nothing to do with physical space. Allah's encompassment is in terms of mercy and knowledge. The Kursi is a locus of manifestation for this knowledge and mercy toward our realm. Neither the relationship of the Kursi nor that of the Throne to Allah is in the nature of occupying space; rather, it is a relationship of Lordship through manifestations such as dominion and governance.\n\nIn verse 256, it is stated: 'There is no compulsion in religion.' This means that compulsion—just as in religion—does not exist in anything within Islam. The subject matter of religion is not involuntary actions, but voluntary actions. Just as belief through compulsion is impossible, an action performed under compulsion is not an act of worship, for 'Actions are judged only by intentions.' All demands of religion must be fulfilled without compulsion, with good will and consent. A person must accept Islam by their own consent; even if the faith of one who believes under compulsion were valid, it would be deficient. The occasion of revelation for the verse occurred when some of the Ansar wished to force their children away from their religions; while this concerned the People of the Book, it carries a general ruling.\n\nThe purpose of jihad is to protect people from compulsion and to elevate the word of Allah by making the true religion prevail. Jihad is not compulsion, but a contest and a test. In the land of Islam, compulsion is forbidden; everyone is free in their religion. Polytheists and the People of the Book can all live with their freedom of religion. However, regarding the Arab polytheists, there is the command, 'Fight them until there is no more fitnah (persecution/discord)'; this is not in the sense of denying them religious freedom, but is a special situation. The view that the verse has been abrogated is weak; it is more widely accepted that it either applies specifically to the People of the Book or retains its general ruling.\n\nThe ruling that 'there is no compulsion in religion' is general, and it covers the period after the right way of Islam has become distinct from error. However, this verse must be considered alongside later verses, such as the command to fight. If there were a contradiction between them, the later would abrogate the earlier; yet it is not certain that this verse was revealed later. Therefore, they may explain and specify one another. Consequently, there is no compulsion within the sphere of Islam's legal rulings; warfare and punishments for crimes fall outside this scope. Furthermore, this ruling remains valid as long as discord (fitnah) is not eliminated and followers of other religions living in the land of Islam do not breach their allegiance.\n\nTaghut derives from the root 'tughyan' (transgression) and refers to anything that is rebellious against Allah and worshipped. Beings such as humans, Satan, idols, and stone altars can be taghut. However, prophets such as Jesus and Ezra are not taghut, because they never consented to polytheism (shirk). The first condition of Tawhid is to reject taghut. In other words, the statement 'there is no deity except Allah' first requires rejecting taghuts. Whoever then believes in Allah has grasped the most trustworthy handhold.\n\nAllah is the ally of those who believe; He brings them out from depths of darkness into the light. Darknesses are many, whereas light is one, and that is the light of Allah. As for those who disbelieve, their allies are the taghuts; they pull them from light into darknesses. These disbelievers will abide eternally in Hell along with their taghuts. A person who does not believe in Allah cannot escape the harassment of taghuts, even if he denies them, and in the end, he is doomed to fall under their sway.\n\nThe debate between Abraham (peace be upon him) and Nimrod shows how a believer should act against taghut. When Abraham was describing his Lord, he said, 'He gives life and causes death.' Nimrod replied, 'I too give life and cause death,' killing one of two men and releasing the other. Thereupon Abraham said, 'Allah brings up the sun from the east, so bring it up from the west,' and Nimrod was confounded. This shows that Allah does not guide the wrongdoing people, and how a believer can defeat taghut with the help of Allah.\n\nA man passed by a town that was fallen into ruin and said, 'How will Allah bring this back to life after its death?' Thereupon, Allah caused him to die for a hundred years, then revived him and asked how long he had remained. The man said, 'A day or less.' Allah showed him that he had remained for a hundred years, that his food and drink had not spoiled, while his donkey had decayed. Then He commanded him to look at the bones; He brought them together and clothed them with flesh. Thus, the man realized that Allah is competent over all things.\n\nAbraham (peace be upon him) said, 'My Lord! Show me how You give life to the dead.' Allah said, 'Have you not believed?' Abraham said, 'I have believed, but I ask so that my heart may be completely reassured.' Thereupon, Allah commanded him to take four birds and tame them to himself, then cut them into pieces, place parts of them on each mountain peak, and call the birds. The birds came running to him. This is an evidence demonstrating Allah's power to bring the dead to life.\n\nIn this section, the commentary regarding the 259th verse of Surah al-Baqarah is addressed. The verse relates that once a person passed by an entirely deserted village whose roofs had caved in and walls had collapsed, and said, 'How will Allah bring this back to life after its death?' The commentators have put forward different views regarding who this person was: some stated that he was Ezra, while others mentioned Jeremiah, a servant of Lot, or another prophet. The location of the village has also been interpreted in different ways; the most famous view is that it was Jerusalem. This event is cited as an example of Allah's power to revive the dead.\n\nThe person's words, 'How will Allah bring this back to life?' may carry different meanings: it could be an expectant anticipation of revival with hope, an admission of human helplessness, or an expression of utter despair. The commentators are of the opinion that this statement was not an absolute denial, but rather a slip of the tongue resulting from a believer's sorrow. For despair is disbelief; however, in moments of extreme grief and helplessness, even prophets may experience a temporary lapse of hope. In such situations, divine help arrives immediately, though it is sometimes deferred in order to discipline the person.\n\nAllah caused that person to die, left him dead for a hundred years, and then revived him. Upon reviving him, He asked, 'How long did you remain?' and the man answered, 'A day or part of a day.' This response shows that personal consciousness continues even after death. Then Allah showed that his food and drink had not spoiled, and demonstrated how the bones of his donkey were joined together and clothed with flesh. This is a living example of resurrection from death. The person reinforced his faith by saying, 'I know that Allah has power over all things.'\n\nIn the second part, the supplication of Abraham, 'My Lord, show me how You give life to the dead,' is addressed. When Allah asked, 'Do you not believe?' Abraham answered, 'I have believed, but I ask so that my heart may be completely reassured.' Thereupon, Allah commanded him to take four birds and become thoroughly familiar with them, then divide them into parts, place a piece on each mountain peak, and then call them. The birds, by Allah's leave, came to life and came to Abraham. This is a miracle concerning Allah's power to give life.\n\nThese two stories emphasize that Allah is the Almighty (al-Aziz) and the All-Wise (al-Hakim). That is, He is both all-powerful over everything and performs His actions with wisdom, linking them to specific causes. Remaining bound to causes does not indicate any incapacity on His part; on the contrary, it is a requirement of His wisdom. The act of giving life occurs sometimes directly in a miraculous manner, and sometimes through a chain of known causes. This signifies the simultaneous manifestation of Allah's attributes of might and wisdom.\n\nFinally, the message these verses give to believers is this: True life and resurrection depend on Allah's will. Whoever has full faith in His power emerges from darkness into light; he finds hope and peace. Abraham's request for 'his heart to be reassured' is the desire to reinforce faith through direct observation. Allah may bestow this station upon whichever of His servants He wills. This is precisely why the verses conclude with the ruling that 'Allah has power over all things.'\n\nWhile discussing the subject of resurrection and giving life, Elmalılı emphasizes that Allah's power is far greater than the capacity of human memory and mind. Even a person's recollection of something forgotten is an example of giving life. Allah's giving of life, however, is far swifter than this. The miracle of Abraham reviving the birds shows that Allah is the Giver of Life (al-Muhyi) and that everything comes into being by His command 'Be.' In this context, spending in the way of Allah is likewise a means of revival and blessing.\n\nIn the 261st verse, the state of those who spend their wealth in the way of Allah is likened to a grain: that grain produces seven ears, in each ear there are a hundred grains, thus seven hundred grains are obtained from a single grain. Allah multiplies further for whom He wills. This example explains that the reward of spending in Allah's cause (infaq) is multiplied manifold. Although the expression 'fi sabilillah' generally denotes expenses made for jihad, this verse applies broadly to every kind of charity and spending. Allah's mercy is vast, and He is All-Knowing.\n\nIn verses 262–264, the conditions for the acceptance of spending (infaq) are explained: Reminding others of one's charity with reproach and causing injury nullifies it. Allah does not accept expenditures made hypocritically by the disbelievers. Believers must not deprive themselves of reward by likening their charity to the ostentation of the disbelievers through reproach and injury. Kind speech and forgiving shortcomings are better than charity followed by injury. Allah is free of all need, Most Forbearing (Halim).\n\nIn verses 265–266, two parables are given: The first is that the condition of those who spend seeking Allah's pleasure and strengthening their own souls is like a beautiful garden on a high hill; a heavy downpour doubles its fruit. The second is the example of someone who has grown old with dependent children and loses their garden to a fiery whirlwind. This conveys that the reward of faith and righteous deeds is Paradise, whereas ostentation and reproach will destroy these blessings.\n\nIn verses 267–274, the details of what to spend from and how to spend are set forth: One must spend from the good things earned and from the best of what the earth produces. Satan threatens with poverty, while Allah promises of His bounty. Whoever is granted wisdom has been granted great good. Charities may be given openly or secretly; giving secretly is better and serves as an expiation for sins. The bashful poor who have dedicated themselves to the cause of Allah might not be widely known; they must be recognized by their features. Those who spend night and day, secretly and openly, have their reward with their Lord; there will be no fear for them, nor will they grieve.\n\nAllah commands the believers to spend from the finest of what they have earned and from what We have brought forth from the earth. They are instructed not to attempt to give foul and worthless things that they themselves would never accept without closing their eyes to them. This command underscores that infaq must be made from that which is purest and most valuable.\n\nThis verse corrects two misconceptions regarding spending. The first is considering wealth merely as the product of labor while neglecting natural resources. The second is regarding capital as the absolute cause of gain and treating labor as worthless. In truth, real capital is the richness of the earth and the bounty of creation. Money is accumulated labor; even when viewed as capital, it is labor by origin. For this reason, spending from the finest earnings has been commanded.\n\nSatan threatens you with poverty and commands you to stinginess and shameful deeds. Allah, however, promises you forgiveness and bounty. Allah's bounty is vast, and He is All-Knowing. He grants multiplied returns in this world and the Hereafter to those who spend. Therefore, one must not be swayed by Satan's whisperings, but rather rely on Allah's promise.\n\nAllah grants wisdom to whom He wills. Whoever is granted wisdom has attained abundant good. Wisdom has many definitions: hitting the mark in speech and deed, the harmony of knowledge and action, grasping the inner reality of created things, understanding Allah's command, and the like. Wisdom is both a divine gift and a virtue that can be acquired through personal endeavor.\n\nThe highest station of wisdom is prophethood. Every prophet is wise (hakim), but not every wise person is a prophet. Attaining wisdom requires a pure intellect and a pure heart. Those who succumb to lusts and satanic whisperings remain deprived of wisdom. This verse encourages the believers to spend from their finest wealth and to seek wisdom.\n\nWisdom rests upon the firm order established by Allah, namely Sunnatullah. Within this order, everything is firmly linked together. Human wisdom consists in understanding this divine order, acting in conformity with it, and producing works beneficial to others. According to Fakhr al-Din al-Razi, Allah's wisdom is creating what serves the benefit of His servants; human wisdom is doing things that benefit others. However, humans must not fall into the delusion of mistaking their own discoveries and inventions for the primary cause.\n\nThere are numerous definitions of wisdom. One is placing everything in its proper place. This holds true for both divine wisdom and human wisdom. Another definition is that wisdom is turning toward good and righteous deeds. Likewise, in governance, a human striving to emulate the Supreme Creator according to human capacity is also wisdom. Moreover, the definition expressed as 'adopting the character of Allah' points to the magnificent character of the Prophet and is identified with the morality of the Quran.\n\nWisdom is the union of knowledge and action. Merely getting stuck in theoretical knowledge is not wisdom, but philosophizing. Beneficial knowledge must be sought, and useless knowledge avoided. Knowledge without action is futile, and action without knowledge cannot be wisdom. Wisdom is therefore the integrated whole of sound knowledge and righteous conduct. The method of Islamic jurisprudence (fiqh) is likewise grounded upon the progression from knowledge to servanthood. The universe is a book of wisdom, and the Quran is the expression of this wisdom in divine speech.\n\nWisdom also possesses a dimension belonging to the heart. Conscience is the soul's realization of itself, and the heart is the focal point of this unity. The divine states of contraction (qabd) and expansion (bast) relate to the tightening and broadening of the heart. A healthy life depends on the balanced continuation of these two states. Wisdom lies in the balance struck between despair and hope, between fear and hope, and in the steadfastness of this harmony.\n\nIn the Quran, wisdom is interpreted in four ways: the admonitions of the Quran, understanding and knowledge, prophethood, and the subtle secrets of the Quran. Fakhr al-Din al-Razi states that all of these signify knowledge. In addition, wisdom denotes knowledge of the abrogating and the abrogated (nasikh and mansukh), the clear and the ambiguous (muhkam and mutashabih), piety (taqwa) in religion, and reverent awe (khashyah). In the end, wisdom is knowledge that leads to beneficial action, beneficial action grounded in knowledge, or soundness in both knowledge and action. The phrase 'abundant good' points to the consummate wisdom born of the union of knowledge and deed.\n\nAllah's bounty and mercy are immensely vast; however, none understand this except the people of wisdom who act upon their knowledge and reflect deeply. Every expenditure made and every vow pledged in the way of Allah or in the way of Satan is known to Allah, and He will recompense it. The wrongdoers have no helpers; those who commit injustice have primarily wronged themselves. Allah's wise order will one day seize and punish them.\n\nThe beginning of wisdom is the fear of Allah. Love of Allah and fear of Him reside together; as nearness increases, fear increases as well. For this reason, Allah first stated, 'None will grasp it except people of deep discernment,' then, 'Indeed, Allah knows it,' and followed this with a three-tiered warning: 'There will be no helpers for the wrongdoers.'\n\nWhen asked whether charities should be given openly or in secret, Allah made known that giving openly is commendable, but giving secretly is better and conceals sins. The word sadaqah denotes truthfulness and perfection. Differing views exist as to whether the charities in the verse refer to the obligatory or the voluntary; the preferred position is that it is more virtuous to give obligatory zakat openly and voluntary charities in secret.\n\nGiving voluntary charities in secret keeps one distant from ostentation, preserves the dignity of the poor, prevents seeking fame and imposing obligation, and multiplies the reward many times over. However, giving openly may be more virtuous if it encourages others. As for obligatory zakat, it ought to be given openly in order to comply with the command and dispel suspicion.\n\nAllah guides whom He wills; you are only a warner. Whatever good you spend is for your own benefit. For this reason, you may also give voluntary charity to the polytheist poor. Obligatory charities, however, are for the poor who are constrained in the way of Allah, unable to engage in trade or travel, appear modest, and do not beg; these are individuals who, like the People of the Bench (Ashab al-Suffah), are devoted to sacred knowledge and worship.\n\nIt is narrated from Abdullah b. Abbas that one day our Prophet (peace be upon him) saw the condition of the People of the Suffah (Ashab al-Suffah), noticed their poverty and hardship, and gave them glad tidings. It is narrated that the verse was revealed upon this incident. The ruling of the verse is general; it encompasses all Muslims who devote themselves for the sake of Allah to paths such as jihad, knowledge, or service, who possess no wealth or property, or who have no time to earn a livelihood. Such people are the foremost priority for charity and spending (infaq). Furthermore, those who perform any act of kindness or generosity should not forget that Allah knows it and will reward it.\n\nThose who spend their wealth night and day, secretly and openly, have a great reward with their Lord; there is no fear for them, nor shall they grieve. Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) gave forty thousand dinars in charity night and day, secretly and openly, and Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) likewise distributed his four silver dirhams in the same manner. Our Prophet (peace be upon him) asked Ali, \"Why did you do this?\" and he replied, \"In order to be worthy of my Lord's promise.\" Although there are different narrations regarding the occasion of revelation of this verse, according to al-Razi, the best view is that the verse was revealed to encourage anyone who gives charity for all times and circumstances.\n\nBeing able to spend night and day, secretly and openly, is the most perfect form of spending (infaq). By accelerating the circulation of wealth, this prevents hoarding and black-marketeering. The individual economy and the general economy are intertwined; when social order deteriorates, the responsibility of the wealthy among the people of faith increases. However, the verse does not command spending all of one's wealth; in the verse (al-Baqarah 2/219), it is commanded to give \"that which is beyond your needs.\" In obligatory charities, the minimum threshold (nisab) must be taken into account, while in voluntary charities, the preservation of capital should be observed. The admonition in Surah al-Isra, \"Do not tie your hand to your neck, nor stretch it out entirely and become wasteful,\" also demonstrates this balance.\n\nAccording to what is narrated from Qatadah, our Prophet (peace be upon him) said, \"Those who amass great wealth are vile,\" and when the Companions asked about exceptions, he said, \"Except those who spend of their wealth to their right, to their left, in front of them, and behind them.\" The verse encourages measured spending that is free from excess. Spending everything is not a requirement; however, total spending, as Ali did, is also permissible. The absolute meaning of the verse should be applied according to needs, times, and individuals. There is no waste in goodness, but spending that leads a person to regret is objectionable. The influence of the social environment is significant in this matter; in an environment hostile to virtuous conduct, the benevolent face great difficulties.\n\nIn the continuation of the verse, usury/interest (riba) is forbidden. Riba literally means excess; in the language of the Sharia, it is an excess in a bilateral contract that remains without a countervalue. The riba widespread among the Arabs of the pre-Islamic era of ignorance (Jahiliyyah) was stipulating an excess payment based on the term of deferral when lending. Allah has made trade lawful and forbidden interest. Those who consume interest will stand like someone beaten by Satan. Whoever desists from interest, their past sins are forgiven; those who return to it are companions of the Fire. Allah blesses charities with increase and destroys interest. Those who believe, do righteous deeds, establish prayer, and pay zakat have their rewards. Believers are commanded to fear Allah and abandon what remains of interest; otherwise, it is declared that war will be waged against them by Allah and His Messenger. If the debtor is in hardship, granting him respite, or even forgiving the debt as charity, is better. On the Day of Resurrection, everyone will be compensated in full for what they have earned.\n\nRiba literally means excess and increase. In the Sharia, it refers to the excess taken on debts—that is, interest. This excess applied to all debts other than interest-free benevolent loans (qard al-hasan) is called riba. Just as in cash transactions, the deferral markup in deferred credit sales (nasi'ah) is also riba. For the six commodities mentioned in the famous hadith (wheat, barley, dates, salt, gold, and silver), an immediate hand-to-hand exchange of the same kind and equal quantity was stipulated, and any excess was deemed riba. Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) stated that riba has obscure forms, and that transactions such as salam (forward sale: paying upfront for future delivery of goods) in animal trading could also be judged as riba.\n\nRiba is more of a societal disease than an individual trouble. It spreads rapidly in impoverished societies and brings society under its sway. At the advent of Islam, thanks to the Muhammadan grace, this development came about within twenty years, and the scourge of riba was wiped away from society. A commerce free of riba was put into practice.\n\nTafsir scholars have enumerated the reasons why riba was declared forbidden. First, riba is taking a person's property without a countervalue. Second, riba keeps people away from genuinely working, earning, and engaging in production. Third, usury causes kindness and mutual assistance among people in the form of 'benevolent loans' (qard al-hasan) to be cut off. Fourth, considering riba permissible grants the rich the opportunity to extract excess wealth from the poor and destitute, which contradicts Allah's mercy. Fifth, the primary reason for riba being forbidden is that it is established by divine textual decree (nass); even if we do not fully know its underlying wisdom, we must recognize that it is forbidden.\n\nIn many respects, riba derails right and justice. Allah gradually diminishes and ultimately destroys riba, which is thought to increase wealth; in contrast, He blesses and multiplies charities, which are thought to decrease wealth. While riba devours like a worm the lives that would produce wealth, charities become reward, life, and blessing. Allah does not love those who are confirmed disbelievers and deeply sinful, persisting regarding lawful and unlawful; He loves those who repent.\n\nThose who believe, do righteous deeds, establish prayer, and pay zakat have their reward with their Lord; there is no fear for them, nor shall they grieve. O you who believe! Fear Allah, and leave what remains of riba. If you do not, know that you will be subjected to a war from Allah and His Messenger. Allah has cursed both the one who consumes riba and the one who pays it. If you repent, your principal is yours; you will neither wrong nor be wronged.\n\nVarious occasions of revelation have been narrated for this verse. Four brothers from the Thaqif tribe, after becoming Muslims, demanded their interest receivables from the Banu Mughirah, and the Prophet sent this verse. In addition, the date trade between Abbas and Uthman b. Affan, as well as the joint usurious partnership between Abbas and Khalid b. al-Walid, are also cited as reasons. During the Farewell Pilgrimage, the Prophet declared that all riba from the era of ignorance was null and void, and the first riba he abolished was that of Abbas, son of Abd al-Muttalib. Contracts made in the abode of war (dar al-harb) are not annulled from their foundation after entering Islam, but they are not permitted to continue.\n\nThose who carry out riba transactions in the Abode of Islam (Dar al-Islam), whether out of denial of religion or with sinful intent, are confronted with war from Allah and His Messenger. If the debtor is destitute, he must be granted time; forgiving the debt as charity is even better. O you who believe! Fear the Day when you will be returned to Allah; everyone will be paid what they have earned, and they will not be wronged. This verse is the very last verse revealed of the Holy Quran; it warns of death and the Day of Resurrection.\n\nThe discourse has transitioned from the subject of spending (infaq) to the rulings on trade and riba, and from there to the rulings on debt. Since debt is a matter upheld by human dignity, Allah has taken it under His own eternal covenant and linked it to the sense of God-consciousness (taqwa), which is the greatest sanction. However, so that piety does not hinder lawful earnings, it has been stated that debts must be recorded in written documents.\n\nIn verse 282, it is commanded that when entering into a debt transaction for a specified term, it must be written down. A scribe who knows how to write should not refuse to write as Allah has taught him. The debtor should dictate, and fearing Allah, his Lord, he should not diminish anything from the right. If the debtor is feeble-minded, a minor, or unable to dictate, his guardian should dictate truthfully. Two men should be present as witnesses; if two men are not available, then one man and two women may witness, so that if one of them forgets, the other may remind her. Witnesses should not refuse when they are called upon, and scribes should not weary of writing down the debt, whether it is small or large, until its term. This is more equitable in the sight of Allah, sounder for testimony, and more conducive to avoiding doubt. However, in immediate on-the-spot transactions, there is no harm in not writing it down. In buying and selling, witnesses should still be taken, and neither the scribe nor the witness should be harmed.\n\nRegarding the testimony of women, the verse stipulates two women in place of one man. The reason for this is that the likelihood of forgetting is greater in women. In a woman's nature, emotionality predominates, which increases susceptibility to influence and forgetting. Moreover, a woman's sense of modesty is strong, and it is not suitable for her to be involved with external commercial affairs. For this reason, the burden of testimony was placed upon two women so that they might remind one another. However, in matters that women know exclusively among themselves, the testimony of a single woman is also valid.\n\nIn verse 283, it is stated that if you are on a journey and cannot find a scribe, taking a pledge will serve in place of a written document. A pledge is also permissible while resident, but it is not recommended when writing is possible. If you trust one another, there is no need to take a pledge; the one who is trusted must fulfill the trust. Concealing testimony burdens the heart with guilt and is among the major sins. Allah knows everything.\n\nIn verse 284, it is stated that whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth belongs to Allah. Whether you disclose what is within yourselves or conceal it, Allah will call you to account for it. Then He forgives whom He wills and punishes whom He wills. Allah is capable of all things. Following the previous rulings on debt and testimony, this verse reminds that everything is within the knowledge of Allah and that people are also held accountable for their intentions.\n\nThere are two narrations regarding the occasion of revelation of the verse: one was revealed concerning concealing testimony and speaking the truth, and the other concerning believers taking disbelievers as allies and imitating them. Whatever is in the heavens and on the earth belongs to Allah; nothing remains hidden from His knowledge. Whether you disclose what is within yourselves or conceal it, Allah will call you to account for it. This verse encompasses all kinds of voluntary and involuntary feelings, thoughts, intentions, and memories within you. However, the context of the verse pertains to evils such as concealing testimony; good deeds seem to be outside this. Moreover, feelings that have firmly settled within you are taken as the basis; fleeting ones are not included. Likewise, voluntary actions are called to account, whereas involuntary ones depend on Allah's will. Ultimately, people must avoid every evil, whether open or hidden, and make good deeds a habit. Allah is capable of all things; He forgives whom He wills and punishes whom He wills.\n\nReturning to the general address at the beginning of the surah, this verse carries the significance of a renewed look at Ayat al-Kursi. While demonstrating the severe punishment for concealing testimony—which is a sin of the heart—it also summarizes the verses, proofs, responsibilities, and rulings within the surah. In particular, it connects faith in the Hereafter to knowing Allah and to His attributes of knowledge, will, and power. These three verses (285-286) are referred to as 'khawatim Surat al-Baqarah' (the concluding verses of Surah al-Baqarah); the first returns to the fifteen verses concerning the disbelievers and hypocrites, while the last two harmonize with the believers and the God-fearing at the beginning of the surah. In this way, it is determined in whom the faith and attributes mentioned at the beginning are actually found.\n\nThe Prophet believed in all that was revealed to him from his Lord, and the believers believed in Allah, His angels, His books, and His messengers. They said, 'We make no distinction between any of Allah's messengers; we hear and obey. Grant us Your forgiveness, our Lord, and to You is the final return.' This verse encompasses all the articles of faith and emphasizes making no distinction among the messengers. According to a narration, when the verse 'whether you disclose what is within yourselves or conceal it' was revealed, it weighed heavily upon the Companions; the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) commanded them to say, 'we hear and obey.' Thereupon, Allah relieved them by revealing the verse stating that He will not burden a soul beyond its capacity.\n\nAllah does not burden any soul beyond its capacity. Every good a person earns is for himself, and every evil he commits is against himself. This verse summarizes the fundamental principle of divine obligation: responsibility is proportional to a person's capacity. Allah desires ease for His servants, not hardship. There are subtle distinctions between the words 'kasb' and 'iktisab'; according to some scholars, kasb is to attain by hitting the mark, whereas iktisab is absolute exertion. According to the first view, good deeds are in a person's favor, and bad deeds are against him. According to the second view, working in accordance with proper procedure is beneficial, while striving blindly is harmful. According to the third view, earning for others is in one's favor, while earning only for oneself is against one. According to the fourth view, enabling others to earn is in one's favor, while living off someone else's earnings is against one.\n\nBecause the believers accepted Allah's commands with graceful obedience, believed in the Hereafter, and sought forgiveness, they were rewarded with this verse. Allah's obligation is not beyond the capacity of His servants; rather, it provides them with mercy and ease. In addition to obligatory duties, there is also encouragement for supererogatory acts of worship and good deeds. In conclusion, if anyone does good, it is to his own benefit, and if he does evil, it is to his own detriment. This principle applies both in this world and the Hereafter. The justice and mercy of Allah require that He does not burden His servants beyond their capacity; otherwise, this would be a punishment. With this wisdom, servants must submit by saying 'we hear and obey' and seek forgiveness from Allah.\n\nAllah gave this response in answer to the supplication of the believers who said, 'We hear, we obey the command, our Lord, we seek Your forgiveness, and to You alone is the return.' These verses show that Allah's speech manifests not only with His Messenger, but also with the community that believes in him, in the form of a conversation and intimate supplication. Through this style, believers find themselves in direct conversation with Allah, and this is expressed as an immense blessing. Those who attain this honor will also be granted the permission to make requests of Allah.\n\nWith this answer, Allah explains the subtlety of obligation and gives the good tidings that no burden beyond their capacity will be placed upon them. At the same time, He indirectly indicates that an obligation beyond one's capacity would have been possible. By explaining the value of earning and that blessing corresponds to hardship, He encourages good deeds that have not been made obligatory. This response also encompasses the content of the sacred hadith, 'My servant continues to draw near to Me with supererogatory acts of worship,' and concludes with a prayer.\n\n\"Our Lord! Do not hold us accountable if we forget or make a mistake.\" Forgetting and making a mistake are of two kinds: in one, a person is excused, while in the other, they are not. For example, one who sees an impurity and forgets to clean it before praying is not excused; however, if they did not see it, they are excused. Likewise, one who shoots at game without considering that a person might be present is not excused. Nor is someone excused who fails to learn religious commandments and does not review them to avoid forgetting. Therefore, the expression \"...beyond what they have the capacity for\" does not encompass all mistakes and instances of forgetfulness; those within one's control do not relieve one of accountability. Evil deeds committed by mistake are as harmful as poison, and before Allah their consequence does not change. For this reason, believers are obligated to avoid forgetfulness and mistakes as much as possible.\n\n\"Our Lord! Do not place upon us a heavy burden as You placed upon those before us.\" Do not subject us to strict rulings and harsh covenants as were imposed upon the Children of Israel. According to tafsir scholars, the Jews were under such obligations as fifty daily prayers, a tax of one-fourth of their wealth, cutting away garments tainted with impurity, capital punishments, and slaying themselves. Believers prayed to be preserved from these hardships, and Allah removed them through the verse in Surah al-A'raf.\n\n\"Our Lord! Lay not on us that which we have no power to bear.\" This prayer is made despite the promise of ease in the preceding verse, because the word capacity can also be used in the sense of endurance, which carries the possibility of pressure. Moreover, an obligation may not be the result of a divine command, but may come through the oppression of transgressors. While believers ask to be protected from every unbearable burden, they know that disbelievers will face unjust burdens and that this is a manifestation of divine justice. Hearts are between two fingers of Allah; supplication is made in order not to deviate from the right path and not to suffer intolerable afflictions.\n\nPardon our sins and grant us forgiveness! Treat us with Your mercy, for You are our Protector. Help us against the community of disbelievers and grant us victory. It is reported that when the Prophet (peace be upon him) prayed with these supplications, Allah stated, \"Indeed, I have done so.\" Surah Al Imran will begin as an answer to this prayer for help and victory.\n\nGreat virtues regarding the last two verses of Surah al-Baqarah have been reported in hadiths. Narrated from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud: \"Whoever recites these two verses from Surah al-Baqarah at night, they will suffice him.\" In a hadith from Abu Dharr: \"Allah concluded Surah al-Baqarah with two verses that are from a treasure beneath the Throne. Learn them and teach them to your children, for they are prayer (salah), supplication, and Quran.\" Umar and Ali would not sleep without reciting these verses. It is also narrated that Gabriel instructed the Prophet (peace be upon him) to say \"amin\" at the end of these verses."